  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$What does it mean for two events to be "mutually exclusive"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They always happen together$m0$, $m0$B) They cannot both happen at the same time$m0$, $m0$C) They have no relation to probability$m0$, $m0$D) They always have equal probability$m0$), 1, $m0$Mutually exclusive events are defined as events that cannot occur simultaneously — if one happens, the other cannot.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$Which of these is an example of independent events?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Drawing two cards from a deck without replacement$m0$, $m0$B) Flipping a coin twice in a row$m0$, $m0$C) Picking a ball from a bag without putting it back and picking again$m0$, $m0$D) Choosing a student from a class and then choosing their sibling$m0$), 1, $m0$Each coin flip is unaffected by the previous one, making them classic independent events.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$What does P(A | B) represent?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The probability of A and B both never happening$m0$, $m0$B) The probability of A occurring, given that B has already occurred$m0$, $m0$C) The probability of B occurring, given A has occurred$m0$, $m0$D) The probability of A or B occurring$m0$), 1, $m0$P(A | B) is read as "the probability of A given B," meaning we assume B has already happened and ask about A's likelihood under that condition.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$Which formula correctly represents conditional probability?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) P(A | B) = P(A) × P(B)$m0$, $m0$B) P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B)$m0$, $m0$C) P(A | B) = P(B) / P(A)$m0$, $m0$D) P(A | B) = P(A) + P(B)$m0$), 1, $m0$Conditional probability is calculated by dividing the probability of both events happening together by the probability of the condition (B) alone.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$What is Bayes' theorem primarily used for?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Calculating the mean of a dataset$m0$, $m0$B) Updating the probability of an event based on new evidence$m0$, $m0$C) Sorting a list of numbers$m0$, $m0$D) Measuring the spread of data$m0$), 1, $m0$Bayes' theorem provides a mathematical way to revise the probability of an event as new, related evidence becomes available.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$In a class of 50 students, 20 play cricket. What is the probability that a randomly chosen student plays cricket?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 0.2$m0$, $m0$B) 0.4$m0$, $m0$C) 0.5$m0$, $m0$D) 2.5$m0$), 1, $m0$Probability = favorable outcomes / total outcomes = 20/50 = 0.4.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$Which ML algorithm is most directly built upon probability and Bayes' theorem?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Linear Regression$m0$, $m0$B) K-Means$m0$, $m0$C) Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$D) PCA$m0$), 2, $m0$The Naive Bayes algorithm applies Bayes' theorem directly, using conditional probabilities to classify data.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-probability-basics-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-probability-basics$m0$, $m0$Why is it a common mistake to assume P(A | B) equals P(B | A)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Because probabilities are always symmetric$m0$, $m0$B) Because these two conditional probabilities generally describe different scenarios and are usually not equal$m0$, $m0$C) Because conditional probability doesn't actually exist$m0$, $m0$D) Because A and B must always be independent$m0$), 1, $m0$P(A | B) and P(B | A) condition on different events, and unless there's a special mathematical relationship between A and B, these values are typically different — this is exactly why Bayes' theorem is needed to convert between them.$m0$, 1, 9)
