values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$What is a scalar?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A grid of numbers$m0$, $m0$B) An ordered list of numbers$m0$, $m0$C) A single number$m0$, $m0$D) A type of loop$m0$), 2, $m0$A scalar is simply one individual number, unlike a vector (a list) or a matrix (a grid).$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$What is the dot product of [1, 2] and [3, 4]?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 7$m0$, $m0$B) 11$m0$, $m0$C) 4$m0$, $m0$D) 24$m0$), 1, $m0$Dot product = (1×3) + (2×4) = 3 + 8 = 11.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$What does the transpose of a matrix do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Doubles every value$m0$, $m0$B) Flips rows into columns and columns into rows$m0$, $m0$C) Deletes the last row$m0$, $m0$D) Converts the matrix into a vector$m0$), 1, $m0$The transpose operation reorganizes a matrix by swapping its rows and columns.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$For matrix multiplication A × B to be valid, what must be true?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A and B must have the same shape$m0$, $m0$B) The number of columns in A must equal the number of rows in B$m0$, $m0$C) Both matrices must be square$m0$, $m0$D) A and B must both be vectors$m0$), 1, $m0$Matrix multiplication requires the inner dimensions to match — columns of the first matrix must equal rows of the second.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$What happens when any compatible matrix is multiplied by the identity matrix?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The result is always a zero matrix$m0$, $m0$B) The matrix remains unchanged$m0$, $m0$C) The matrix is transposed$m0$, $m0$D) An error always occurs$m0$), 1, $m0$The identity matrix behaves like the number 1 in multiplication — multiplying any compatible matrix by it leaves the original matrix unchanged.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$Which NumPy function correctly performs true matrix multiplication (not element-wise)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `np.add()`$m0$, $m0$B) `np.matmul()`$m0$, $m0$C) `*` operator on plain arrays$m0$, $m0$D) `np.subtract()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`np.matmul()` (or the `@` operator) performs proper matrix multiplication, following the row-by-column dot product rule.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$What is the result of a dot product between two vectors?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A new vector of the same length$m0$, $m0$B) A single number (scalar)$m0$, $m0$C) A matrix$m0$, $m0$D) Always zero$m0$), 1, $m0$The dot product always produces a single scalar value, formed by summing the products of corresponding elements.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$Which of these best describes a vector?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A single number$m0$, $m0$B) An ordered list of numbers$m0$, $m0$C) A function that returns text$m0$, $m0$D) A type of loop structure$m0$), 1, $m0$A vector is simply an ordered sequence of numbers, often representing feature values or coordinates.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$Why is the distinction between element-wise multiplication and matrix multiplication important in ML?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) There is no real difference between them$m0$, $m0$B) They produce different results and are used for different purposes$m0$, $m0$C) Element-wise multiplication is always faster and preferred$m0$, $m0$D) Matrix multiplication is only used for text data$m0$), 1, $m0$These two operations serve very different purposes and yield different outputs, so confusing them can silently lead to completely incorrect calculations in ML code.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-linear-algebra-basics-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-linear-algebra-basics$m0$, $m0$If matrix A has shape (3, 2) and matrix B has shape (2, 4), what is the shape of A × B?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) (3, 4)$m0$, $m0$B) (2, 2)$m0$, $m0$C) (4, 3)$m0$, $m0$D) (3, 2)$m0$), 0, $m0$The resulting matrix takes the outer dimensions: rows from A (3) and columns from B (4), giving shape (3, 4).$m0$, 1, 9)
