values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Which type of ML uses data that includes both inputs and known correct outputs?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Reinforcement Learning$m0$, $m0$D) None of the above$m0$), 1, $m0$Supervised Learning specifically relies on labeled data — inputs paired with their correct, known outputs.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Customer segmentation, where groups are discovered without predefined labels, is an example of which ML type?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Reinforcement Learning$m0$, $m0$D) Deep Learning only$m0$), 1, $m0$Since there are no predefined group labels, discovering natural groupings in the data is a classic Unsupervised Learning task.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$What kind of feedback does a Reinforcement Learning agent receive?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A fixed labeled dataset$m0$, $m0$B) No feedback of any kind$m0$, $m0$C) Rewards or penalties based on its actions$m0$, $m0$D) Direct correct answers for every input$m0$), 2, $m0$Reinforcement Learning agents learn through rewards and penalties received while interacting with an environment, rather than fixed labeled examples.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Which ML type would best suit predicting house prices from a dataset of past sales with known prices?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Reinforcement Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$D) None of the above$m0$), 2, $m0$Since the dataset includes known correct outputs (past sale prices), this is a Supervised Learning problem.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Why is Reinforcement Learning often computationally expensive?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It requires no data at all$m0$, $m0$B) It can require many interactions/trials with an environment to learn effectively$m0$, $m0$C) It always uses labeled data$m0$, $m0$D) It never requires any computation$m0$), 1, $m0$Reinforcement Learning agents often need to try many actions across many episodes before learning an effective strategy, which can demand significant computation.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$What is a major challenge specific to Supervised Learning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It never requires any data$m0$, $m0$B) Obtaining sufficient labeled data can be expensive or time-consuming$m0$, $m0$C) It cannot be evaluated at all$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with images$m0$), 1, $m0$Since Supervised Learning relies on labeled examples, acquiring enough accurately labeled data is often a significant practical challenge.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Which of these best describes an Unsupervised Learning goal?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Predicting a known label for new data$m0$, $m0$B) Maximizing long-term reward through actions$m0$, $m0$C) Discovering hidden patterns or structure in unlabeled data$m0$, $m0$D) Memorizing training examples exactly$m0$), 2, $m0$Unsupervised Learning is centered on finding structure, patterns, or groupings within data that has no labels.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$A game-playing AI that learns by receiving points for good moves is an example of:$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Reinforcement Learning$m0$, $m0$D) None of the above$m0$), 2, $m0$Learning through a reward signal (points) received from interacting with an environment (the game) is a defining characteristic of Reinforcement Learning.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Which question would help you decide if a problem is Supervised Learning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Do I have any data at all?$m0$, $m0$B) Do I have labeled examples with known correct answers?$m0$, $m0$C) Is my dataset extremely large?$m0$, $m0$D) Does my problem involve images?$m0$), 1, $m0$The presence of labeled examples (known correct outputs) is the defining test for whether a problem is Supervised Learning.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-types-of-machine-learning-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-types-of-machine-learning$m0$, $m0$Which statement correctly distinguishes the three ML types?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) All three types require labeled data$m0$, $m0$B) Supervised uses labels, Unsupervised has no labels, Reinforcement learns via rewards from interaction$m0$, $m0$C) Unsupervised Learning always uses more data than Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$D) Reinforcement Learning is simply another name for Supervised Learning$m0$), 1, $m0$This correctly captures the core distinguishing feature of each type — labeled data, unlabeled data, and reward-based interaction, respectively.$m0$, 1, 9)
