values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$What is required for Supervised Learning to work?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Only unlabeled data$m0$, $m0$B) Labeled data (features paired with known correct outputs)$m0$, $m0$C) No data at all$m0$, $m0$D) A reward signal from an environment$m0$), 1, $m0$Supervised Learning specifically depends on having labeled examples, where the correct output for each input is already known.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Predicting whether an email is "spam" or "not spam" is an example of:$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Regression$m0$, $m0$B) Classification$m0$, $m0$C) Clustering$m0$, $m0$D) Reinforcement Learning$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the output is a category (spam or not spam), this is a classification task.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Predicting the exact price of a house is an example of:$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Classification$m0$, $m0$B) Regression$m0$, $m0$C) Clustering$m0$, $m0$D) Reinforcement Learning$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the output is a continuous numeric value (a price), this is a regression task.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$What does `model.fit(X, y)` do in a supervised learning context?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Deletes the training data$m0$, $m0$B) Trains the model using the features (X) and known labels (y)$m0$, $m0$C) Displays a chart of the data$m0$, $m0$D) Converts labels into features$m0$), 1, $m0$`.fit()` is the training step, where the model learns the relationship between the input features and their corresponding known labels.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a common limitation of Supervised Learning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It requires no data whatsoever$m0$, $m0$B) It requires labeled data, which can be expensive or difficult to obtain$m0$, $m0$C) It cannot be evaluated using any metrics$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with images$m0$), 1, $m0$Since Supervised Learning depends on labeled examples, acquiring sufficient accurately labeled data is a common real-world challenge.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Which metric would typically be used to evaluate a regression model?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Accuracy$m0$, $m0$B) Precision$m0$, $m0$C) Mean Absolute Error (MAE)$m0$, $m0$D) F1 Score$m0$), 2, $m0$MAE (Mean Absolute Error) is a standard evaluation metric for regression tasks, measuring how far predictions are from actual continuous values.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Why is it important to test a supervised model on data it hasn't seen during training?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It isn't important at all$m0$, $m0$B) To check whether the model generalizes well to new data$m0$, $m0$C) To make the model train faster$m0$, $m0$D) To reduce the size of the dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$Testing on unseen data verifies that the model has learned generalizable patterns, rather than simply memorizing the training examples.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Which of the following is an example of a supervised classification problem?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Grouping customers by shopping behavior with no predefined categories$m0$, $m0$B) Predicting whether a tumor is malignant or benign, based on labeled medical data$m0$, $m0$C) Training a robot to walk through trial and error$m0$, $m0$D) Reducing the number of features in a dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the outcome (malignant/benign) is a known category learned from labeled medical data, this is a supervised classification problem.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$What is the main difference between classification and regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Classification predicts categories; regression predicts continuous numeric values$m0$, $m0$B) They are exactly the same$m0$, $m0$C) Classification requires no data$m0$, $m0$D) Regression only works with text data$m0$), 0, $m0$This is the fundamental distinction: classification outputs discrete categories, while regression outputs continuous numbers.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-supervised-learning-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-supervised-learning$m0$, $m0$Which module of this course focuses primarily on specific supervised learning algorithms?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Module 5 — Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Module 4 — Supervised Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Module 9 — Practical Projects only$m0$, $m0$D) Module 1 — Prerequisites$m0$), 1, $m0$Module 4 is dedicated specifically to supervised learning algorithms such as Linear Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, and more.$m0$, 1, 9)
