insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What is the primary purpose of the training set?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To give a final, unbiased performance estimate$m0$, $m0$B) To teach the model by fitting its parameters$m0$, $m0$C) To store unused data permanently$m0$, $m0$D) To visualize the data only$m0$), 1, $m0$The training set is the data the model directly learns from, adjusting its parameters based on these examples.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What is the validation set primarily used for?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Final, one-time performance evaluation$m0$, $m0$B) Tuning hyperparameters and comparing models during development$m0$, $m0$C) Storing raw, unprocessed data$m0$, $m0$D) Replacing the need for a training set$m0$), 1, $m0$The validation set allows experimentation — such as hyperparameter tuning and model comparison — without touching the test set.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$Why should the test set be evaluated only once, at the very end?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It doesn't matter how many times it's evaluated$m0$, $m0$B) Repeated evaluation and adjustment based on it can lead to misleadingly optimistic results$m0$, $m0$C) The test set is not actually useful for anything$m0$, $m0$D) Test sets are always larger than training sets$m0$), 1, $m0$Using the test set repeatedly for adjustments effectively leaks information from it into your model, undermining its purpose as an honest, final check.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function is commonly used to split data into training and testing sets?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `train_test_split()`$m0$, $m0$B) `split_data()`$m0$, $m0$C) `divide_dataset()`$m0$, $m0$D) `create_sets()`$m0$), 0, $m0$`train_test_split()` is the standard Scikit-learn function used to split data into separate training and testing (or validation) portions.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What does `random_state=42` do in `train_test_split()`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Randomly changes the split every time the code runs$m0$, $m0$B) Ensures the same split is reproduced every time the code runs$m0$, $m0$C) Deletes 42% of the data$m0$, $m0$D) Has no effect at all$m0$), 1, $m0$Setting a fixed `random_state` ensures the data split is reproducible — running the same code again produces the identical split.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What is a common risk when working with very small datasets and a fixed train/validation/test split?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) There is no risk at all$m0$, $m0$B) Too little data may remain for reliable training or evaluation$m0$, $m0$C) The model automatically becomes perfect$m0$, $m0$D) Test sets always become larger than training sets$m0$), 1, $m0$With small datasets, splitting into three parts can leave each portion too small to give reliable training or evaluation results — Cross-Validation is often used to address this.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$Which set would you use to decide between two different models during development?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Test set$m0$, $m0$B) Validation set$m0$, $m0$C) Neither set$m0$, $m0$D) A brand-new, unrelated dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$The validation set is specifically intended for making development-time decisions, such as comparing models or tuning hyperparameters.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What best describes "data leakage" in this context?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Losing physical data files$m0$, $m0$B) Information from the test set improperly influencing model development$m0$, $m0$C) A type of missing value in the dataset$m0$, $m0$D) A synonym for cross-validation$m0$), 1, $m0$Data leakage occurs when information that should remain unseen (like test set results) inadvertently influences the model-building process, invalidating fair evaluation.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a typical training/validation/test split ratio?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 10% / 10% / 80%$m0$, $m0$B) 70% / 15% / 15%$m0$, $m0$C) 0% / 0% / 100%$m0$, $m0$D) 33% / 33% / 33% always required$m0$), 1, $m0$A common, though not strictly fixed, split ratio allocates the majority of data to training, with smaller, roughly equal portions for validation and testing.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-training-validation-testing-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-training-validation-testing$m0$, $m0$What is the key characteristic that distinguishes the test set from the validation set?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The test set is used repeatedly throughout development$m0$, $m0$B) The test set is used exactly once, at the very end, for a final unbiased evaluation$m0$, $m0$C) The validation set is always larger$m0$, $m0$D) There is no meaningful difference between them$m0$), 1, $m0$Unlike the validation set (used repeatedly during development), the test set is reserved for a single, final, unbiased evaluation of the completed model.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

