values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$What are "features" in a supervised learning dataset?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The known correct outputs$m0$, $m0$B) The input variables used to make a prediction$m0$, $m0$C) A type of loop in Python$m0$, $m0$D) A visualization technique$m0$), 1, $m0$Features are the input information (independent variables) the model uses to make its prediction.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$What is a "label" in a supervised learning dataset?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A type of feature$m0$, $m0$B) The known, correct output the model is trying to predict$m0$, $m0$C) A column that is always removed before training$m0$, $m0$D) A synonym for "features"$m0$), 1, $m0$The label represents the known correct answer or target value that the model learns to predict from the features.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$Which of these is an example of a categorical feature?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Age in years$m0$, $m0$B) House price$m0$, $m0$C) City name$m0$, $m0$D) Square footage$m0$), 2, $m0$A city name represents a category (a label-like value) rather than a numeric measurement, making it a categorical feature.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$In Pandas, which of these correctly selects a single column as the label?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `df[["passed"]]`$m0$, $m0$B) `df["passed"]`$m0$, $m0$C) `df.passed()`$m0$, $m0$D) Both A and B always behave identically$m0$), 1, $m0$Using single square brackets, `df["passed"]`, returns the column as a `Series`, which is the standard format for the label (`y`).$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$Why is choosing good features often just as important as choosing the right algorithm?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Features have no real effect on model performance$m0$, $m0$B) Poor or irrelevant features can significantly hurt model performance regardless of the algorithm used$m0$, $m0$C) Algorithms always fix bad feature choices automatically$m0$, $m0$D) Features and algorithms are entirely unrelated concepts$m0$), 1, $m0$Even the best algorithm will struggle if given poor-quality, irrelevant, or misleading features — feature quality has a major impact on overall model performance.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$What is a common mistake when separating features and labels?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Always using too few features$m0$, $m0$B) Accidentally including the label column as one of the features$m0$, $m0$C) Never using numerical features$m0$, $m0$D) Selecting labels using square brackets$m0$), 1, $m0$Accidentally leaving the label column inside the features (`X`) can let the model "cheat" by learning directly from the answer, leading to misleadingly good but unrealistic performance.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$What is the standard notation used for features and labels, respectively?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `y` for features, `X` for labels$m0$, $m0$B) `X` for features, `y` for labels$m0$, $m0$C) `A` for features, `B` for labels$m0$, $m0$D) There is no standard notation$m0$), 1, $m0$By widespread convention in ML code and libraries like Scikit-learn, `X` refers to the features and `y` refers to the label/target.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$Which of the following is an example of a numerical feature?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) House color$m0$, $m0$B) Neighborhood name$m0$, $m0$C) Square footage$m0$, $m0$D) Pass/fail outcome$m0$), 2, $m0$Square footage is a direct numeric measurement, making it a numerical feature.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$In a dataset predicting whether a loan will default, which of these is most likely the label?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Applicant's income$m0$, $m0$B) Applicant's credit score$m0$, $m0$C) Whether the loan was defaulted on or not$m0$, $m0$D) Applicant's employment type$m0$), 2, $m0$Since this is the known outcome the model is trying to predict, "whether the loan was defaulted on" is the label — the other options are input features.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-features-labels-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-features-labels$m0$, $m0$Why must categorical features often be specially processed before use in ML models?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Categorical features are never useful$m0$, $m0$B) Most ML algorithms require numeric input, so categories must typically be encoded into numbers$m0$, $m0$C) Categorical features are always labels, not features$m0$, $m0$D) Categorical features cannot exist in a Pandas DataFrame$m0$), 1, $m0$Since most ML algorithms operate mathematically on numbers, categorical features usually need to be encoded into a numeric form before being used — a process covered fully in Module 3.$m0$, 1, 9)
