values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$What is the main goal of data cleaning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To make a dataset larger$m0$, $m0$B) To fix incorrect, inconsistent, or irrelevant data before analysis$m0$, $m0$C) To convert all data into images$m0$, $m0$D) To remove all columns from a dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$Data cleaning focuses on correcting and standardizing data so that it's accurate and consistent before further analysis or modeling.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$Which method would you use to convert a column of numeric-looking text into actual integers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `.str.upper()`$m0$, $m0$B) `.astype(int)`$m0$, $m0$C) `.drop()`$m0$, $m0$D) `.fillna()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`.astype(int)` explicitly converts a column's data type to integer, fixing cases where numbers were incorrectly stored as text.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$Which method combination would correctly clean up `"  KABIR  "` into `"Kabir"`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `.str.upper()`$m0$, $m0$B) `.str.strip().str.title()`$m0$, $m0$C) `.astype(str)`$m0$, $m0$D) `.replace()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`.str.strip()` removes extra whitespace, and `.str.title()` standardizes capitalization to title case, producing `"Kabir"`.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$Why might `"M"`, `"male"`, and `"Male"` cause problems in a dataset?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are always treated as identical automatically$m0$, $m0$B) They may be treated as three separate categories instead of one$m0$, $m0$C) Pandas cannot store text data$m0$, $m0$D) They will cause the program to crash immediately$m0$), 1, $m0$Without cleaning, these inconsistent labels are treated as distinct categories, which can distort analysis and confuse ML models.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$Which Pandas method would you use to standardize inconsistent category labels into one consistent value?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `.replace()`$m0$, $m0$B) `.sort_values()`$m0$, $m0$C) `.describe()`$m0$, $m0$D) `.shape`$m0$), 0, $m0$`.replace()` allows you to map multiple inconsistent values to a single standardized label.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a valid reason to drop a column during data cleaning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The column is highly relevant to the problem$m0$, $m0$B) The column provides no useful analytical value (e.g., an internal-only ID)$m0$, $m0$C) The column contains numeric data$m0$, $m0$D) The column has no missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$Columns that don't contribute meaningful information to the analysis or model are good candidates for removal during cleaning.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-data-cleaning-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-data-cleaning$m0$, $m0$What does `df.dtypes` help verify after cleaning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of rows in the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) Whether each column now has the correct, expected data type$m0$, $m0$C) The average value of numeric columns$m0$, $m0$D) The number of missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$`df.dtypes` shows the data type of each column, letting you confirm that cleaning steps (like type conversion) were applied successfully.$m0$, 1, 6),
