values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Which Pandas method detects missing values in a DataFrame?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `df.dropna()`$m0$, $m0$B) `df.isnull()`$m0$, $m0$C) `df.fillna()`$m0$, $m0$D) `df.describe()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`isnull()` returns `True`/`False` for each cell, indicating whether the value is missing.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$What does `df.dropna()` do by default?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Fills missing values with zero$m0$, $m0$B) Removes rows containing any missing values$m0$, $m0$C) Removes all columns regardless of missing values$m0$, $m0$D) Converts missing values into the string "NaN"$m0$), 1, $m0$By default, `dropna()` removes any row that contains at least one missing value.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Which imputation strategy is generally more robust to outliers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Mean imputation$m0$, $m0$B) Median imputation$m0$, $m0$C) Random imputation$m0$, $m0$D) Maximum value imputation$m0$), 1, $m0$Median is less affected by extreme values than mean, making it a safer choice for imputation when outliers are present.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Which imputation strategy would you typically use for a categorical column?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Mean$m0$, $m0$B) Median$m0$, $m0$C) Most frequent value (mode)$m0$, $m0$D) Standard deviation$m0$), 2, $m0$Since categorical data has no numeric average, filling missing values with the most frequently occurring category (mode) is the standard approach.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$What does `SimpleImputer(strategy="mean").fit_transform(X)` do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Deletes all missing values without replacement$m0$, $m0$B) Calculates the mean from the data and fills missing values with it$m0$, $m0$C) Converts all values into their mean regardless of whether they were missing$m0$, $m0$D) Removes the entire column$m0$), 1, $m0$`fit_transform()` calculates the specified statistic (here, the mean) from the available data and uses it to fill in missing values.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Why should an imputer typically be fit only on the training data?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It doesn't matter where it's fit$m0$, $m0$B) To avoid data leakage from the test set into the imputation process$m0$, $m0$C) Because imputers cannot be used on test data at all$m0$, $m0$D) Because test data never contains missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$Fitting the imputer only on training data ensures that information from the test set doesn't influence the imputation values, preserving a fair evaluation.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$What is a key risk of using deletion (dropping rows) to handle missing values?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It always improves model accuracy$m0$, $m0$B) It can discard potentially valuable data, especially if missing values are common$m0$, $m0$C) It is impossible to implement in Pandas$m0$, $m0$D) It always increases the dataset size$m0$), 1, $m0$Deleting rows/columns removes any other valid data they contained, which can be wasteful if missing values are widespread.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Which fill method would be most appropriate for time-series data with missing values?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Mean imputation$m0$, $m0$B) Mode imputation$m0$, $m0$C) Forward fill (`ffill`)$m0$, $m0$D) Dropping the entire dataset$m0$), 2, $m0$Forward fill carries the last known valid value forward, which is a natural fit for sequential, time-ordered data.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$What does `df.isnull().sum()` return?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The total number of rows in the DataFrame$m0$, $m0$B) The count of missing values in each column$m0$, $m0$C) The mean of each column$m0$, $m0$D) The data types of each column$m0$), 1, $m0$This combination first flags missing values (`isnull()`) and then sums them per column, giving a count of missing values for each one.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-handling-missing-values-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-handling-missing-values$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a legitimate reason to prefer imputation over deletion?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) When missing values are extremely rare$m0$, $m0$B) When dropping affected rows/columns would discard too much valuable data$m0$, $m0$C) When the dataset has no missing values at all$m0$, $m0$D) When you want to intentionally reduce dataset size$m0$), 1, $m0$Imputation preserves the overall dataset size, making it preferable when a significant portion of the data would otherwise be lost through deletion.$m0$, 1, 9)
