  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$Which Pandas method detects duplicate rows in a DataFrame?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `df.dropna()`$m0$, $m0$B) `df.duplicated()`$m0$, $m0$C) `df.unique()`$m0$, $m0$D) `df.describe()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`duplicated()` flags rows that exactly match a previous row, returning `True`/`False` for each row.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$Which method removes duplicate rows from a DataFrame?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `df.remove_duplicates()`$m0$, $m0$B) `df.drop_duplicates()`$m0$, $m0$C) `df.clean_duplicates()`$m0$, $m0$D) `df.filter_duplicates()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`drop_duplicates()` is the standard Pandas method for removing duplicate rows.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$By default, which occurrence of a duplicate does `drop_duplicates()` keep?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The last occurrence$m0$, $m0$B) The first occurrence$m0$, $m0$C) A random occurrence$m0$, $m0$D) None — it removes all occurrences$m0$), 1, $m0$By default, `keep="first"` is used, meaning the first occurrence of a duplicate is retained and later occurrences are removed.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$What does the `subset` parameter in `duplicated()` or `drop_duplicates()` allow you to do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Only work with numeric columns$m0$, $m0$B) Detect duplicates based on specific columns rather than the entire row$m0$, $m0$C) Automatically fill missing values$m0$, $m0$D) Sort the DataFrame$m0$), 1, $m0$The `subset` parameter restricts duplicate detection to only the specified column(s), rather than requiring an exact match across every column.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$Why might undetected duplicate rows distort an ML model?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They have no effect on the model whatsoever$m0$, $m0$B) They can cause certain patterns to be overrepresented, skewing what the model learns$m0$, $m0$C) They automatically improve model accuracy$m0$, $m0$D) They only affect categorical columns$m0$), 1, $m0$Repeated rows can cause a model to give undue weight to those particular data points, distorting the patterns it learns from the data.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$What does `df.duplicated().sum()` return?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The sum of all numeric values in the DataFrame$m0$, $m0$B) The total count of duplicate rows detected$m0$, $m0$C) The number of columns in the DataFrame$m0$, $m0$D) The average value of each column$m0$), 1, $m0$Since `duplicated()` returns `True`/`False` per row, summing these values gives the total number of duplicate rows found.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$What does setting `keep=False` in `drop_duplicates()` do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Keeps only the first occurrence of each duplicate$m0$, $m0$B) Keeps only the last occurrence of each duplicate$m0$, $m0$C) Removes all occurrences of any row that had duplicates$m0$, $m0$D) Has no effect on the result$m0$), 2, $m0$`keep=False` removes every occurrence of a row that appeared more than once, rather than keeping any single copy.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$Which scenario would benefit most from subset-based duplicate detection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Checking whether any two rows are identical across every single column$m0$, $m0$B) Checking specifically for repeated customer IDs, regardless of minor differences in other columns$m0$, $m0$C) Detecting missing values$m0$, $m0$D) Detecting outliers in a numeric column$m0$), 1, $m0$Subset-based detection is ideal when duplication matters based on a specific key (like customer ID), even if other columns don't match exactly.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$What is a potential cause of duplicate rows in a real-world dataset?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Data cleaning was performed too thoroughly$m0$, $m0$B) A form was accidentally submitted twice, or data was merged from multiple overlapping sources$m0$, $m0$C) The dataset was never loaded correctly$m0$, $m0$D) All datasets always contain zero duplicates$m0$), 1, $m0$Duplicate rows commonly arise from repeated submissions, system glitches, or merging multiple data sources that contain overlapping records.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-removing-duplicates-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-removing-duplicates$m0$, $m0$Why is it good practice to check for duplicates before checking for missing values or outliers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It doesn't matter what order these checks are performed in$m0$, $m0$B) Duplicate rows are always more important to fix than missing values$m0$, $m0$C) Order doesn't strictly matter, but understanding your dataset's true structure early helps guide the rest of the cleaning process$m0$, $m0$D) Missing values must always be handled before checking for duplicates$m0$), 2, $m0$While there's no strict required order, gaining an early, accurate picture of the dataset's true structure (free of artificial duplication) helps make later cleaning decisions more reliable.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

