insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$What is an outlier?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The most common value in a dataset$m0$, $m0$B) A data point that differs dramatically from the rest of the dataset$m0$, $m0$C) A missing value$m0$, $m0$D) A duplicate row$m0$), 1, $m0$Outliers are defined as observations that lie unusually far from the typical range of values in a dataset.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$In the IQR method, how is the interquartile range calculated?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Maximum − Minimum$m0$, $m0$B) Q3 − Q1$m0$, $m0$C) Mean − Median$m0$, $m0$D) Standard deviation × 2$m0$), 1, $m0$IQR is calculated as the difference between the 75th percentile (Q3) and the 25th percentile (Q1).$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Using the standard IQR method, which formula defines the upper bound for detecting outliers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Q3 + 1.5 × IQR$m0$, $m0$B) Q1 − 1.5 × IQR$m0$, $m0$C) Mean + 2 × Standard Deviation$m0$, $m0$D) Q3 × 1.5$m0$), 0, $m0$Values above `Q3 + 1.5 × IQR` are commonly flagged as outliers using the standard IQR method.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$What does a Z-score measure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The exact value of a data point$m0$, $m0$B) How many standard deviations a value is from the mean$m0$, $m0$C) The number of missing values in a dataset$m0$, $m0$D) The total number of rows in a dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$The Z-score standardizes a value by expressing how far it is from the mean, measured in standard deviations.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Which method assumes the data is roughly normally distributed?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The IQR method$m0$, $m0$B) The Z-score method$m0$, $m0$C) Neither method makes this assumption$m0$, $m0$D) Both methods equally assume this$m0$), 1, $m0$The Z-score method relies on mean and standard deviation, which are most meaningful when data follows a roughly normal (bell-curve) distribution.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Why should outliers not always be automatically removed?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Outliers never occur in real datasets$m0$, $m0$B) Some outliers represent genuinely important, rare events rather than errors$m0$, $m0$C) Removing outliers is always illegal$m0$, $m0$D) Outliers have no effect on statistics or models$m0$), 1, $m0$Certain outliers, such as fraudulent transactions, carry valuable information and should be investigated rather than blindly discarded.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Which visualization is commonly used to spot outliers at a glance?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Pie chart$m0$, $m0$B) Boxplot$m0$, $m0$C) Bar chart$m0$, $m0$D) Line chart$m0$), 1, $m0$A boxplot visually displays the interquartile range, median, and clearly marks points that fall outside the typical range as outliers.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$What commonly happens to statistics like the mean when significant outliers are present?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The mean becomes completely unaffected$m0$, $m0$B) The mean can be heavily skewed toward the outlier's value$m0$, $m0$C) The mean always decreases$m0$, $m0$D) Outliers only affect the median, never the mean$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the mean incorporates every value equally, a few extreme outliers can pull it substantially away from what's "typical" for most of the data.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Which of these datasets would likely benefit MOST from careful outlier investigation rather than automatic removal?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A dataset of weekly grocery bills for typical households$m0$, $m0$B) A dataset of financial transactions being screened for fraud$m0$, $m0$C) A dataset with no variability at all$m0$, $m0$D) A dataset containing only categorical data$m0$), 1, $m0$In fraud detection, outliers often represent exactly the events of interest, making careful investigation far more valuable than simple removal.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-outlier-detection-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-outlier-detection$m0$, $m0$Which threshold is commonly used with the Z-score method to flag outliers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Z-score beyond ±1$m0$, $m0$B) Z-score beyond ±2 or ±3$m0$, $m0$C) Z-score exactly equal to 0$m0$, $m0$D) Z-score below 0 only$m0$), 1, $m0$A Z-score threshold of ±2 or ±3 standard deviations from the mean is commonly used to flag values as potential outliers.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

