insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$What is the main goal of Feature Selection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To add as many new features as possible$m0$, $m0$B) To choose the most relevant, useful subset of existing features$m0$, $m0$C) To convert categorical data into numbers$m0$, $m0$D) To remove all missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$Feature Selection focuses on identifying and retaining only the most useful existing features, removing irrelevant or redundant ones.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$Which Pandas method computes the correlation matrix of a DataFrame?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `df.corr()`$m0$, $m0$B) `df.correlate()`$m0$, $m0$C) `df.relation()`$m0$, $m0$D) `df.match()`$m0$), 0, $m0$`df.corr()` calculates pairwise correlation coefficients between all numeric columns in a DataFrame.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$What does a correlation value close to 0 between a feature and the target suggest?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A very strong linear relationship$m0$, $m0$B) A weak or negligible linear relationship$m0$, $m0$C) The feature is definitely useless$m0$, $m0$D) The feature contains missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$A correlation near 0 indicates little to no linear relationship, though it doesn't rule out potentially useful non-linear relationships.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$Why might two highly correlated features be considered redundant?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They always cause errors when used together$m0$, $m0$B) They likely provide very similar/overlapping information$m0$, $m0$C) High correlation means one feature is definitely wrong$m0$, $m0$D) Correlation has no bearing on redundancy$m0$), 1, $m0$When two features are highly correlated, they tend to carry similar information, meaning one might be sufficient without significant loss of predictive power.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$What is a limitation of relying solely on correlation for feature selection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Correlation always perfectly captures every type of relationship$m0$, $m0$B) Correlation only captures linear relationships, missing potential non-linear ones$m0$, $m0$C) Correlation cannot be calculated in Python$m0$, $m0$D) Correlation only works with categorical data$m0$), 1, $m0$Correlation coefficients measure linear relationships specifically, so a feature with a genuinely useful non-linear relationship to the target might be incorrectly judged as unimportant.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$Which of these features would likely be a strong candidate for removal via feature selection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A feature very strongly correlated with the target$m0$, $m0$B) A feature that is nearly constant across all rows (very low variance)$m0$, $m0$C) A feature central to the business problem being solved$m0$, $m0$D) A feature with no missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$A feature that barely varies across rows provides little useful information for distinguishing between different outcomes, making it a strong candidate for removal.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$What is the key difference between Feature Selection and Feature Engineering?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are exactly the same process$m0$, $m0$B) Feature Selection chooses among existing features; Feature Engineering creates new features$m0$, $m0$C) Feature Engineering only removes features; Feature Selection only adds features$m0$, $m0$D) Feature Selection is only used in Deep Learning$m0$), 1, $m0$Feature Selection narrows down existing features to the most useful subset, while Feature Engineering focuses on creating new, potentially more informative features.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$Why is domain knowledge important when performing feature selection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It isn't important at all — statistics alone are always sufficient$m0$, $m0$B) Statistics alone might miss important context about why a feature matters$m0$, $m0$C) Domain knowledge always overrides all statistical evidence$m0$, $m0$D) Domain knowledge is only relevant for categorical features$m0$), 1, $m0$Purely statistical measures like correlation can miss important real-world context, so combining them with domain expertise leads to better-informed feature selection decisions.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$What should you do after removing a candidate feature based on correlation analysis?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Nothing further is needed$m0$, $m0$B) Validate that the change doesn't hurt (and ideally improves) actual model performance$m0$, $m0$C) Immediately add 10 new random features$m0$, $m0$D) Delete the entire dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$Correlation-based hints should be validated in practice by measuring whether the resulting model actually performs as well or better without the removed feature.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-feature-selection-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-feature-selection$m0$, $m0$In which module of this course is Feature Engineering (creating new features) covered in depth?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Module 3 — Data Preprocessing$m0$, $m0$B) Module 5 — Unsupervised Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Module 7 — Improving ML Models$m0$, $m0$D) Module 1 — Prerequisites$m0$), 2, $m0$While Feature Selection is covered here in Module 3, Feature Engineering (creating new, potentially more useful features) is covered in depth in Module 7.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

