insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function is used to split data into training and testing sets?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `split_dataset()`$m0$, $m0$B) `train_test_split()`$m0$, $m0$C) `divide_data()`$m0$, $m0$D) `create_split()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`train_test_split()` is the standard Scikit-learn function for this purpose.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$What does `test_size=0.2` specify?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 20% of the data is reserved for training$m0$, $m0$B) 20% of the data is reserved for testing$m0$, $m0$C) The dataset will have exactly 20 rows$m0$, $m0$D) 2% of the data is reserved for testing$m0$), 1, $m0$`test_size=0.2` means 20% of the full dataset is set aside as the test set, with the remaining 80% used for training.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$What is the purpose of the `random_state` parameter?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real effect$m0$, $m0$B) It ensures the same split is reproduced every time the code runs$m0$, $m0$C) It automatically balances imbalanced classes$m0$, $m0$D) It removes missing values before splitting$m0$), 1, $m0$Setting a fixed `random_state` value ensures that the same random split occurs each time the code is executed, supporting reproducibility.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$Why is `stratify=y` particularly useful for imbalanced classification problems?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no special benefit for imbalanced data$m0$, $m0$B) It ensures the training and test sets maintain the same class proportions as the original data$m0$, $m0$C) It automatically increases the size of the minority class$m0$, $m0$D) It removes the minority class entirely$m0$), 1, $m0$Stratification preserves the original class distribution across both the training and test sets, preventing an unrepresentative split, especially important when one class is much rarer than another.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$What could happen without stratification on a highly imbalanced dataset?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The split would always be perfectly balanced regardless$m0$, $m0$B) The test set might end up with very few or even zero examples of the minority class$m0$, $m0$C) The training process would fail immediately$m0$, $m0$D) All data would be duplicated$m0$), 1, $m0$A plain random split can, by chance, allocate very few minority-class examples to the test set, making evaluation unreliable — stratification helps prevent this.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$In what order should train-test splitting and feature scaling typically be performed?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Scaling should always happen before splitting$m0$, $m0$B) Splitting should generally happen first, then scalers should be fit on the training set only$m0$, $m0$C) Order doesn't matter at all$m0$, $m0$D) Scaling and splitting must always happen simultaneously$m0$), 1, $m0$Performing the split first, then fitting scalers only on the training data, prevents information from the test set from leaking into the scaling process.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$What does `train_test_split(X, y, ...)` return?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A single combined DataFrame$m0$, $m0$B) Four separate outputs: X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test$m0$, $m0$C) Only the training set$m0$, $m0$D) Only the test set$m0$), 1, $m0$The function returns four separate arrays/DataFrames, splitting both the features and labels into training and testing portions.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$Why is a plain random split generally NOT appropriate for time-series data?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Time-series data cannot be split at all$m0$, $m0$B) A random split can mix future and past data inappropriately, leaking future information into training$m0$, $m0$C) Time-series data never needs to be split$m0$, $m0$D) Random splits always work perfectly for time-series data$m0$), 1, $m0$In time-series problems, a random split risks training on future data and testing on past data (or vice versa), which doesn't reflect how the model would actually be used in practice — a chronological split is needed instead.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a limitation of a single train_test_split, compared to Cross-Validation?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It is always more computationally expensive$m0$, $m0$B) A single split's results can vary somewhat depending on which specific random split occurred$m0$, $m0$C) It cannot be used with Scikit-learn$m0$, $m0$D) It requires stratification in every single case$m0$), 1, $m0$Since a single split depends on one particular random partitioning of the data, its evaluation results can vary somewhat compared to the more robust, averaged results from Cross-Validation.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-train-test-split-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-train-test-split$m0$, $m0$What is a key benefit of setting `random_state` when comparing multiple models?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It makes all models perform identically$m0$, $m0$B) It ensures each model is evaluated on the exact same train/test split, enabling fair comparison$m0$, $m0$C) It removes the need for a test set entirely$m0$, $m0$D) It automatically selects the best model$m0$), 1, $m0$Using the same `random_state` across experiments ensures every model is trained and tested on identical data splits, making performance comparisons fair and meaningful.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

