values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$What type of task is Logistic Regression primarily used for?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Regression (predicting continuous values)$m0$, $m0$B) Classification (predicting discrete categories)$m0$, $m0$C) Clustering$m0$, $m0$D) Dimensionality reduction$m0$), 1, $m0$Despite its name, Logistic Regression is a classification algorithm, most commonly used for binary classification tasks.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$What does the sigmoid function do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Converts probabilities into raw linear values$m0$, $m0$B) Squashes any real number into a range between 0 and 1$m0$, $m0$C) Removes missing values from a dataset$m0$, $m0$D) Calculates the mean of a dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$The sigmoid function transforms any real-valued input into an output between 0 and 1, making it suitable for representing probabilities.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$What is the default decision threshold used to convert a probability into a class prediction?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 0.1$m0$, $m0$B) 0.5$m0$, $m0$C) 1.0$m0$, $m0$D) 0.9$m0$), 1, $m0$By default, a probability of 0.5 or higher is classified as class 1, and below 0.5 as class 0.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn method returns the actual predicted probabilities (not just class labels)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `.fit()`$m0$, $m0$B) `.predict()`$m0$, $m0$C) `.predict_proba()`$m0$, $m0$D) `.score()`$m0$), 2, $m0$`.predict_proba()` returns the underlying probability estimates for each class, while `.predict()` returns only the final hard class label.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$If a model predicts P(y=1) = 0.3, what class would be predicted using the default threshold?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Class 1$m0$, $m0$B) Class 0$m0$, $m0$C) Both classes simultaneously$m0$, $m0$D) No prediction is possible$m0$), 1, $m0$Since 0.3 is below the default threshold of 0.5, the model would predict class 0.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a real-world application of Logistic Regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Predicting a continuous house price$m0$, $m0$B) Detecting whether an email is spam or not spam$m0$, $m0$C) Grouping customers with no known labels$m0$, $m0$D) Reducing the number of features in a dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$Spam detection is a binary classification problem (spam or not spam), a classic use case for Logistic Regression.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$What is a key limitation of Logistic Regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It cannot output probabilities$m0$, $m0$B) It assumes a roughly linear relationship between features and the log-odds of the outcome$m0$, $m0$C) It can only be used with exactly one feature$m0$, $m0$D) It cannot be implemented in Python$m0$), 1, $m0$Logistic Regression's performance can suffer on problems where the true relationship between features and the outcome is strongly non-linear.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$Why might you adjust the default 0.5 decision threshold in a medical diagnosis context?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Thresholds can never be adjusted$m0$, $m0$B) To reduce the risk of missing true positive cases (e.g., failing to detect a disease), even at the cost of more false alarms$m0$, $m0$C) To make the model faster$m0$, $m0$D) To convert the problem into regression$m0$), 1, $m0$In high-stakes contexts like medical diagnosis, it's often preferable to lower the threshold to catch more true positive cases, even if it means more false positives.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$What does the letter `z` typically represent in the Logistic Regression formula `P(y=1) = 1/(1+e^-z)`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The final predicted probability$m0$, $m0$B) The linear combination of input features and their coefficients$m0$, $m0$C) The number of training examples$m0$, $m0$D) The learning rate$m0$), 1, $m0$`z` is the linear combination (`b₀ + b₁x₁ + ... + bₙxₙ`) computed before being passed through the sigmoid function to produce a probability.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-logistic-regression-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-logistic-regression$m0$, $m0$Which statement correctly distinguishes Logistic Regression from Linear Regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Logistic Regression predicts continuous values; Linear Regression predicts categories$m0$, $m0$B) Logistic Regression predicts probabilities/categories; Linear Regression predicts continuous values$m0$, $m0$C) They are exactly the same algorithm$m0$, $m0$D) Neither algorithm can be implemented in Scikit-learn$m0$), 1, $m0$This is the fundamental distinction — Logistic Regression handles classification (via probabilities), while Linear Regression handles continuous numeric prediction.$m0$, 1, 9)
