insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$How does KNN make a prediction for a new data point?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) By fitting a straight line through the data$m0$, $m0$B) By looking at the k nearest neighbors and using majority vote or averaging$m0$, $m0$C) By applying the sigmoid function$m0$, $m0$D) By calculating the mean of the entire dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$KNN identifies the k closest training points to the new data point and bases its prediction on those neighbors specifically.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$Which distance metric is most commonly used in KNN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Manhattan distance only$m0$, $m0$B) Euclidean distance$m0$, $m0$C) Alphabetical distance$m0$, $m0$D) Categorical distance$m0$), 1, $m0$Euclidean distance (straight-line distance) is the most commonly used metric for measuring similarity between points in KNN.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$Why is feature scaling especially important for KNN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real importance for KNN$m0$, $m0$B) Features with larger numeric ranges can dominate distance calculations unless scaled$m0$, $m0$C) Scaling converts categorical data automatically$m0$, $m0$D) It removes the need for choosing a value of k$m0$), 1, $m0$Since KNN relies directly on distances between feature values, unscaled features with larger ranges can disproportionately influence the result.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$What happens if `k=1` in KNN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The model becomes very robust to noise$m0$, $m0$B) The prediction depends entirely on just the single closest neighbor, making it very sensitive to noise/outliers$m0$, $m0$C) The model considers the entire dataset equally$m0$, $m0$D) KNN cannot function with k=1$m0$), 1, $m0$With k=1, a single potentially noisy or unusual neighbor can entirely determine the prediction, making the model highly sensitive to that data point.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$For a KNN classification problem, how is the final predicted class determined?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) By averaging all neighbor values$m0$, $m0$B) By majority vote among the k nearest neighbors$m0$, $m0$C) By choosing the neighbor with the highest index$m0$, $m0$D) By random selection among neighbors$m0$), 1, $m0$In classification, KNN predicts whichever class appears most frequently among the k nearest neighbors.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$What is a key computational drawback of KNN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It requires no data at all$m0$, $m0$B) Prediction can be slow for large datasets, since distances must be calculated to every training point$m0$, $m0$C) It cannot handle more than one feature$m0$, $m0$D) It always requires a GPU$m0$), 1, $m0$Since every prediction requires comparing the new point to all training points, KNN's prediction time can grow significantly with dataset size.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$Which of these best describes KNN's "training" process?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It fits complex mathematical coefficients through gradient descent$m0$, $m0$B) It essentially just stores the training data for later comparison$m0$, $m0$C) It requires no training data at all$m0$, $m0$D) It builds a decision tree structure$m0$), 1, $m0$Unlike algorithms that learn explicit parameters, KNN is instance-based — it simply retains the training data and performs calculations at prediction time.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$What does the hyperparameter `k` represent in KNN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of features in the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) The number of nearest neighbors considered for each prediction$m0$, $m0$C) The number of training examples total$m0$, $m0$D) The learning rate of the algorithm$m0$), 1, $m0$`k` specifies how many of the closest training points should be considered when making a prediction for a new data point.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$Why might choosing a very large value of `k` be problematic?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It always causes an error$m0$, $m0$B) It can over-smooth predictions, potentially ignoring genuinely local patterns in the data$m0$, $m0$C) It makes KNN faster than any other algorithm$m0$, $m0$D) It has no downsides whatsoever$m0$), 1, $m0$A very large k considers many neighbors, potentially diluting genuinely relevant local patterns with less relevant, more distant points.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-nearest-neighbors$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements KNN for classification tasks?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `LinearRegression`$m0$, $m0$B) `KNeighborsClassifier`$m0$, $m0$C) `LogisticRegression`$m0$, $m0$D) `DecisionTreeRegressor`$m0$), 1, $m0$`KNeighborsClassifier` is the standard Scikit-learn class for applying KNN to classification problems.$m0$, 1, 9)
