values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$What does a leaf node in a Decision Tree represent?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A further splitting question$m0$, $m0$B) A final prediction$m0$, $m0$C) The root of the tree$m0$, $m0$D) A missing value$m0$), 1, $m0$Leaf nodes are the endpoints of a Decision Tree, representing the final predicted class or value once no further splits occur.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$What does Gini Impurity measure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The accuracy of the entire model$m0$, $m0$B) How mixed the classes are within a node$m0$, $m0$C) The number of features in the dataset$m0$, $m0$D) The depth of the tree$m0$), 1, $m0$Gini Impurity quantifies how mixed or "impure" the classes are within a given node, with 0 meaning perfectly pure.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$What does "Information Gain" measure in the context of Decision Trees?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The total number of leaves in the tree$m0$, $m0$B) How much a particular split reduces impurity$m0$, $m0$C) The training time of the model$m0$, $m0$D) The number of missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$Information Gain quantifies the improvement in purity achieved by a specific split, guiding the algorithm's choice of where to split.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$Why are Decision Trees prone to overfitting?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They cannot process numeric data$m0$, $m0$B) Without limits, they can keep splitting until they perfectly memorize the training data, including noise$m0$, $m0$C) They only work with categorical data$m0$, $m0$D) They require far too little data to train$m0$), 1, $m0$Left unconstrained, a Decision Tree can create highly specific splits that fit training data (including its noise) too closely, hurting generalization to new data.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$Which parameter helps control a Decision Tree's tendency to overfit?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `n_neighbors`$m0$, $m0$B) `max_depth`$m0$, $m0$C) `learning_rate`$m0$, $m0$D) `test_size`$m0$), 1, $m0$Limiting `max_depth` restricts how many levels of splits the tree can make, helping prevent it from overfitting to the training data.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$What is a key advantage of Decision Trees over many other ML algorithms?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are always the most accurate algorithm available$m0$, $m0$B) They are highly interpretable, with a clear, visualizable decision path$m0$, $m0$C) They never require any tuning$m0$, $m0$D) They cannot be visualized$m0$), 1, $m0$Decision Trees allow you to trace the exact sequence of decisions leading to any prediction, making them one of the most interpretable ML algorithms.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a common limitation of individual Decision Trees?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They can be unstable, with small data changes leading to very different tree structures$m0$, $m0$B) They cannot handle categorical data$m0$, $m0$C) They require extensive feature scaling$m0$, $m0$D) They can only make one single prediction ever$m0$), 0, $m0$Decision Trees can be quite sensitive to small variations in the training data, sometimes producing a substantially different tree structure as a result.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$Given a node with Gini Impurity = 0, what does this indicate?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The node is highly mixed between classes$m0$, $m0$B) The node is perfectly pure (contains only one class)$m0$, $m0$C) The node has the maximum possible impurity$m0$, $m0$D) The tree has reached its maximum depth$m0$), 1, $m0$A Gini Impurity of 0 indicates that all samples in that node belong to a single class, representing perfect purity.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function helps visualize a trained Decision Tree's structure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `plt.scatter()`$m0$, $m0$B) `plot_tree()`$m0$, $m0$C) `plt.hist()`$m0$, $m0$D) `df.describe()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`plot_tree()` from `sklearn.tree` generates a visual diagram of the tree's learned splits and structure.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-decision-trees-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-decision-trees$m0$, $m0$What algorithm, covered in the next topic, builds directly upon multiple Decision Trees to improve performance and stability?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) K-Nearest Neighbors$m0$, $m0$B) Random Forest$m0$, $m0$C) Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$D) Support Vector Machines$m0$), 1, $m0$Random Forest combines many individual Decision Trees into an ensemble, addressing many of a single tree's limitations, as covered in the next topic.$m0$, 1, 9)
