values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$What does SVM aim to maximize when finding a separating boundary?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of support vectors$m0$, $m0$B) The margin between the boundary and the closest points of each class$m0$, $m0$C) The total number of training examples$m0$, $m0$D) The depth of the decision boundary$m0$), 1, $m0$SVM specifically searches for the hyperplane that maximizes the margin — the distance to the nearest points of each class.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$What are "support vectors"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) All the data points in the training set$m0$, $m0$B) The specific data points closest to the separating hyperplane, which determine its position$m0$, $m0$C) A type of kernel function$m0$, $m0$D) The predicted output labels$m0$), 1, $m0$Support vectors are the critical, closest data points from each class that directly influence where the maximum-margin hyperplane is placed.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$What is the purpose of the "kernel trick" in SVM?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To remove missing values from the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) To allow SVM to handle data that isn't linearly separable, by implicitly mapping it to a higher-dimensional space$m0$, $m0$C) To speed up training on very large datasets exclusively$m0$, $m0$D) To convert continuous labels into categories$m0$), 1, $m0$The kernel trick enables SVM to find separating boundaries for data that wouldn't be linearly separable in its original feature space.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a commonly used SVM kernel?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Gini kernel$m0$, $m0$B) RBF (Radial Basis Function) kernel$m0$, $m0$C) Entropy kernel$m0$, $m0$D) Euclidean kernel$m0$), 1, $m0$RBF is one of the most commonly used non-linear kernels in SVM, alongside linear and polynomial kernels.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$Why is feature scaling important for SVM?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no effect on SVM's performance$m0$, $m0$B) SVM's margin and boundary calculations are sensitive to the relative scale of different features$m0$, $m0$C) Scaling is only relevant for classification, not SVM specifically$m0$, $m0$D) SVM cannot process numeric data without scaling$m0$), 1, $m0$Since SVM relies on geometric distances (similar to KNN) to determine the optimal margin, unscaled features can distort these calculations.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$What does `model.n_support_` reveal in a trained Scikit-learn SVM?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The total accuracy of the model$m0$, $m0$B) The number of support vectors identified for each class$m0$, $m0$C) The learning rate used during training$m0$, $m0$D) The number of features in the dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$`n_support_` shows how many training points from each class were identified as support vectors, defining the learned boundary.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$Which SVM kernel would be most appropriate for data that is roughly linearly separable?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) RBF kernel$m0$, $m0$B) Linear kernel$m0$, $m0$C) Polynomial kernel of very high degree$m0$, $m0$D) No kernel is ever needed$m0$), 1, $m0$A linear kernel directly seeks a straight-line/flat hyperplane boundary, which is appropriate when the data is already reasonably separable this way.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$What is a common limitation of SVM?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It can be computationally expensive to train on very large datasets$m0$, $m0$B) It cannot handle more than 2 features$m0$, $m0$C) It always requires categorical labels$m0$, $m0$D) It has no hyperparameters to tune$m0$), 0, $m0$SVM's training process can become computationally demanding as dataset size grows significantly, particularly with non-linear kernels.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$Why might SVM generalize well to new, unseen data?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It memorizes every training example exactly$m0$, $m0$B) Maximizing the margin tends to create a boundary that is robust to small variations in new data$m0$, $m0$C) It ignores all training data during prediction$m0$, $m0$D) It always achieves 100% accuracy on any dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$A wide margin means the decision boundary has more "breathing room," making it less sensitive to minor variations or noise in new data points near the boundary.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-support-vector-machines-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-support-vector-machines$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class is commonly used to implement SVM for classification?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `SVC`$m0$, $m0$B) `SVR`$m0$, $m0$C) `KNeighborsClassifier`$m0$, $m0$D) `RandomForestClassifier`$m0$), 0, $m0$`SVC` (Support Vector Classifier) is the standard Scikit-learn class for applying SVM to classification tasks.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

