values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$What mathematical theorem does Naive Bayes directly build upon?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The Pythagorean theorem$m0$, $m0$B) Bayes' Theorem$m0$, $m0$C) The Central Limit Theorem$m0$, $m0$D) Newton's laws of motion$m0$), 1, $m0$Naive Bayes applies Bayes' Theorem to calculate the probability of each class given the observed features.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$What is the "naive" assumption made by Naive Bayes?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) That all features are perfectly correlated with each other$m0$, $m0$B) That all features are conditionally independent of each other, given the class$m0$, $m0$C) That the dataset has no missing values$m0$, $m0$D) That there is only ever one class$m0$), 1, $m0$Naive Bayes simplifies the math by assuming features don't influence each other once the class is known — an assumption that's rarely perfectly true but often works well in practice.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$Which Naive Bayes variant is commonly used for text classification based on word counts?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Gaussian Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$B) Multinomial Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$C) Bernoulli Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$D) Linear Naive Bayes$m0$), 1, $m0$Multinomial Naive Bayes is specifically designed for count-based data, such as word frequencies in text classification.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$Which Naive Bayes variant assumes numeric features follow a normal distribution?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Multinomial Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$B) Bernoulli Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$C) Gaussian Naive Bayes$m0$, $m0$D) None of them make this assumption$m0$), 2, $m0$Gaussian Naive Bayes specifically models continuous numeric features as following a normal (bell-curve) distribution within each class.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$How does Naive Bayes decide on a final predicted class?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) By choosing the class with the lowest probability score$m0$, $m0$B) By choosing the class with the highest combined probability score$m0$, $m0$C) By randomly selecting a class$m0$, $m0$D) By always predicting the most common class in the dataset, ignoring the features$m0$), 1, $m0$Naive Bayes calculates a combined probability score for each class and predicts whichever class has the highest score.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$Why is Naive Bayes particularly popular for spam email detection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It cannot process text data at all$m0$, $m0$B) It's fast, works well with high-dimensional word-based features, and handles this kind of classification effectively$m0$, $m0$C) It requires no training data whatsoever$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with numeric image data$m0$), 1, $m0$Naive Bayes' speed and effectiveness with high-dimensional, sparse text data make it a natural fit for tasks like spam detection.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$What is a key limitation of the independence assumption in Naive Bayes?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It always leads to perfect predictions$m0$, $m0$B) It's rarely perfectly true in real-world data, which can affect probability accuracy (though classifications often remain correct)$m0$, $m0$C) It makes the algorithm impossible to implement$m0$, $m0$D) It only applies to numeric data$m0$), 1, $m0$Real-world features are often correlated in some way, violating the independence assumption — yet Naive Bayes frequently still produces useful, accurate classifications despite this simplification.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements Gaussian Naive Bayes?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `MultinomialNB`$m0$, $m0$B) `GaussianNB`$m0$, $m0$C) `BernoulliNB`$m0$, $m0$D) `LinearNB`$m0$), 1, $m0$`GaussianNB` is the Scikit-learn class specifically designed for continuous numeric features assumed to follow a normal distribution.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$What does `model.predict_proba()` return for a Naive Bayes classifier?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The raw training data$m0$, $m0$B) The final normalized probability for each possible class$m0$, $m0$C) The number of features used$m0$, $m0$D) The model's training time$m0$), 1, $m0$`predict_proba()` returns the calculated, normalized probability for each class, allowing insight beyond just the final hard classification.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-naive-bayes-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-naive-bayes$m0$, $m0$Why is Naive Bayes considered fast compared to many other ML algorithms?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It requires solving complex iterative optimization problems$m0$, $m0$B) It relies on straightforward probability calculations rather than complex iterative training$m0$, $m0$C) It only works with very small datasets$m0$, $m0$D) It doesn't actually process any data$m0$), 1, $m0$Naive Bayes calculates probabilities directly from the data's statistics, avoiding the more computationally intensive iterative optimization used by many other algorithms.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

