insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What does a centroid represent in K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A randomly chosen data point that never changes$m0$, $m0$B) The average position of all points currently assigned to a cluster$m0$, $m0$C) The total number of clusters$m0$, $m0$D) The distance between two clusters$m0$), 1, $m0$A centroid is recalculated at each iteration as the mean position of all data points currently assigned to that particular cluster.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What are the two main steps repeated in each iteration of K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Scaling and encoding$m0$, $m0$B) Assignment (to nearest centroid) and update (recalculating centroids)$m0$, $m0$C) Splitting and merging$m0$, $m0$D) Training and testing$m0$), 1, $m0$K-Means alternates between assigning points to their nearest centroid and updating each centroid based on its newly assigned points.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What does the Elbow Method help determine?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The best distance metric to use$m0$, $m0$B) A reasonable value for `k` (the number of clusters)$m0$, $m0$C) Whether the dataset has any missing values$m0$, $m0$D) The exact centroid positions in advance$m0$), 1, $m0$The Elbow Method examines how WCSS changes across different values of `k`, helping identify a good balance between simplicity and cluster fit.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What does "inertia" (WCSS) measure in K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of iterations completed$m0$, $m0$B) The sum of squared distances between points and their assigned centroids$m0$, $m0$C) The total number of features in the dataset$m0$, $m0$D) The accuracy of the clustering result$m0$), 1, $m0$Inertia (WCSS) quantifies how tightly grouped points are within their assigned clusters — lower values indicate tighter, more cohesive clusters.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$Why must `k` be specified before running K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) K-Means automatically determines the ideal number of clusters$m0$, $m0$B) The algorithm's core process (assigning to centroids) requires knowing how many centroids to place$m0$, $m0$C) K-Means doesn't actually use `k` at all$m0$, $m0$D) `k` refers to the number of features, not clusters$m0$), 1, $m0$Since K-Means initializes and iteratively updates a fixed number of centroids, that number (`k`) must be decided before the algorithm begins.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What is a key limitation of K-Means regarding cluster shape?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It works equally well with any cluster shape$m0$, $m0$B) It assumes roughly spherical, evenly-sized clusters, and struggles with irregular shapes$m0$, $m0$C) It can only handle exactly 2 clusters$m0$, $m0$D) It requires categorical data only$m0$), 1, $m0$K-Means' distance-based assignment approach works best when clusters are roughly round and similarly sized; it performs poorly on irregular or very differently sized/shaped clusters.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$Why does Scikit-learn's `KMeans` include an `n_init` parameter?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To specify the number of clusters$m0$, $m0$B) To run the algorithm multiple times with different random initializations and choose the best result$m0$, $m0$C) To control feature scaling automatically$m0$, $m0$D) To set the maximum number of iterations$m0$), 1, $m0$Since K-Means results can depend on the initial random centroid placement, `n_init` runs the algorithm multiple times, helping avoid poor results from an unlucky initialization.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$What does `model.cluster_centers_` return after fitting a K-Means model?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The cluster assignments for each point$m0$, $m0$B) The final centroid positions for each cluster$m0$, $m0$C) The total inertia value$m0$, $m0$D) The original unclustered data$m0$), 1, $m0$`cluster_centers_` provides the coordinates of each cluster's final centroid after the algorithm has converged.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$Why is feature scaling important before applying K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no effect on K-Means results$m0$, $m0$B) K-Means relies on distance calculations, which can be distorted by features with very different ranges$m0$, $m0$C) Scaling automatically determines the best value of `k`$m0$, $m0$D) K-Means cannot process numeric data without scaling$m0$), 1, $m0$Similar to KNN, K-Means' reliance on distance calculations means unscaled features with larger ranges can disproportionately influence cluster assignments.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-k-means-clustering-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-k-means-clustering$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements K-Means clustering?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `KMeans`$m0$, $m0$B) `KNeighborsClassifier`$m0$, $m0$C) `DecisionTreeClassifier`$m0$, $m0$D) `LogisticRegression`$m0$), 0, $m0$`KMeans` is the standard Scikit-learn class for applying the K-Means clustering algorithm.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

