values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What does the `eps` parameter define in DBSCAN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The minimum number of clusters$m0$, $m0$B) The neighborhood radius used to determine if points are "neighbors"$m0$, $m0$C) The maximum number of iterations$m0$, $m0$D) The number of features in the dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$`eps` specifies the maximum distance within which two points are considered neighbors of each other.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What defines a "core point" in DBSCAN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Any randomly selected point in the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) A point with at least `min_samples` neighbors within `eps` distance$m0$, $m0$C) A point that is farthest from all other points$m0$, $m0$D) The very first point processed by the algorithm$m0$), 1, $m0$A core point must have a sufficient number of neighboring points (at least `min_samples`) within the specified `eps` radius.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$How does DBSCAN label points that don't belong to any cluster?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) With a label of 0$m0$, $m0$B) With a label of -1$m0$, $m0$C) DBSCAN cannot identify such points$m0$, $m0$D) With the label of the nearest cluster regardless$m0$), 1, $m0$DBSCAN specifically uses the label -1 to mark points classified as noise, distinguishing them from any actual cluster.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What is a key advantage of DBSCAN over K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) DBSCAN always trains faster on very large datasets$m0$, $m0$B) DBSCAN can identify noise/outliers and handle irregularly shaped clusters$m0$, $m0$C) DBSCAN requires specifying the number of clusters, which K-Means does not$m0$, $m0$D) DBSCAN cannot use distance calculations$m0$), 1, $m0$Unlike K-Means, DBSCAN explicitly identifies noise points and can discover clusters of arbitrary shapes, rather than assuming roughly spherical groups.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What is a "border point" in DBSCAN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A point with enough neighbors to be a core point itself$m0$, $m0$B) A point that isn't a core point itself, but falls within reach of a core point's neighborhood$m0$, $m0$C) A point that belongs to no cluster whatsoever$m0$, $m0$D) The centroid of a cluster$m0$), 1, $m0$Border points don't meet the core point density requirement themselves, but are still included in a cluster because they're within reach of a qualifying core point.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$Why can choosing `eps` and `min_samples` be challenging in DBSCAN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) These parameters have no real effect on the clustering result$m0$, $m0$B) The right values often depend on the specific dataset and require experimentation$m0$, $m0$C) DBSCAN automatically selects perfect values every time$m0$, $m0$D) These parameters are only relevant for K-Means, not DBSCAN$m0$), 1, $m0$Since data density and scale vary between datasets, finding suitable `eps` and `min_samples` values typically requires some trial and error or domain knowledge.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$Which of these datasets would likely benefit most from DBSCAN rather than K-Means?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Perfectly round, evenly-sized, well-separated clusters$m0$, $m0$B) Data with irregularly shaped clusters and some clear outliers$m0$, $m0$C) A dataset with exactly 2 data points$m0$, $m0$D) A dataset with no variability at all$m0$), 1, $m0$DBSCAN's density-based approach is specifically well-suited for irregular shapes and datasets containing genuine outliers, unlike K-Means' spherical cluster assumption.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What happens to a point that has fewer than `min_samples` neighbors and is NOT within reach of any core point?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It becomes a core point automatically$m0$, $m0$B) It is labeled as noise$m0$, $m0$C) It is forced into the nearest cluster regardless$m0$, $m0$D) It becomes the new centroid$m0$), 1, $m0$A point that fails both the core point condition and isn't near any core point is classified as noise, meaning it doesn't belong to any cluster.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements DBSCAN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `KMeans`$m0$, $m0$B) `DBSCAN`$m0$, $m0$C) `AgglomerativeClustering`$m0$, $m0$D) `LogisticRegression`$m0$), 1, $m0$`DBSCAN` is the standard Scikit-learn class implementing this density-based clustering algorithm.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-dbscan-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-dbscan$m0$, $m0$What potential limitation does DBSCAN have regarding cluster density?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It performs perfectly regardless of any density variation$m0$, $m0$B) It can struggle when clusters have significantly different densities, since a single `eps` may not suit all regions$m0$, $m0$C) It requires every cluster to have exactly the same density$m0$, $m0$D) Density has no bearing on DBSCAN's results$m0$), 1, $m0$Since DBSCAN uses a single, fixed `eps` value across the entire dataset, it can struggle to correctly identify clusters when different regions of the data have very different densities.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

