insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$What is the main goal of PCA?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To increase the number of features in a dataset$m0$, $m0$B) To reduce dimensionality while preserving as much variance (information) as possible$m0$, $m0$C) To remove all missing values$m0$, $m0$D) To classify data into predefined categories$m0$), 1, $m0$PCA's core purpose is compressing data into fewer dimensions while retaining as much of its original variance/information as possible.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$What is a "principal component"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) An original feature from the dataset, unchanged$m0$, $m0$B) A new, artificially constructed feature formed from a weighted combination of original features$m0$, $m0$C) A missing value in the dataset$m0$, $m0$D) A type of clustering algorithm$m0$), 1, $m0$Principal components are new features specifically constructed to capture maximum variance, calculated as combinations of the original features.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$What does the "explained variance ratio" indicate?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The accuracy of a classification model$m0$, $m0$B) How much of the original data's variance/information is captured by each principal component$m0$, $m0$C) The number of clusters found$m0$, $m0$D) The total number of features in the original dataset$m0$), 1, $m0$The explained variance ratio quantifies how much of the original dataset's total variance each principal component accounts for.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$Why is feature scaling important before applying PCA?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real effect on PCA results$m0$, $m0$B) PCA is sensitive to feature scale, and unscaled features with larger ranges could dominate the components$m0$, $m0$C) Scaling automatically determines the number of components to keep$m0$, $m0$D) PCA cannot process numeric data without scaling$m0$), 1, $m0$Since PCA identifies directions of maximum variance, unscaled features with naturally larger numeric ranges could disproportionately influence the resulting principal components.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$If the first two principal components have an explained variance ratio of 0.7 and 0.2 respectively, what is their combined explained variance?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 0.9 (90%)$m0$, $m0$B) 0.14 (14%)$m0$, $m0$C) 1.4 (140%)$m0$, $m0$D) 0.5 (50%)$m0$), 0, $m0$Adding the individual explained variance ratios: 0.7 + 0.2 = 0.9, meaning these two components together explain 90% of the original data's variance.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pca-principal-component-analysis$m0$, $m0$How does PCA differ from Feature Selection?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are exactly the same technique$m0$, $m0$B) PCA creates new artificial features; Feature Selection chooses a subset of existing original features$m0$, $m0$C) Feature Selection always creates more features than the original dataset$m0$, $m0$D) PCA never changes the number of features$m0$), 1, $m0$This is the key distinction — PCA constructs entirely new components, while Feature Selection retains a subset of the actual original features.$m0$, 1, 5),
