values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$Why can accuracy be misleading on imbalanced datasets?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Accuracy is never a valid metric$m0$, $m0$B) A model can achieve high accuracy simply by always predicting the majority class$m0$, $m0$C) Accuracy always equals precision$m0$, $m0$D) Imbalanced datasets cannot be evaluated at all$m0$), 1, $m0$With heavily imbalanced classes, always predicting the majority class can yield high accuracy while completely failing to identify the minority class.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$What does Precision measure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The proportion of actual positives correctly identified$m0$, $m0$B) The proportion of predicted positives that were actually correct$m0$, $m0$C) The overall proportion of correct predictions$m0$, $m0$D) The balance between false positives and false negatives$m0$), 1, $m0$Precision specifically evaluates how many of the model's positive predictions were genuinely correct.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$What does Recall measure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The proportion of predicted positives that were correct$m0$, $m0$B) The proportion of actual positive cases that were correctly identified$m0$, $m0$C) The total number of predictions made$m0$, $m0$D) The model's training time$m0$), 1, $m0$Recall specifically measures how many of the true positive cases the model successfully caught.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$What is a False Negative?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Correctly predicting the negative class$m0$, $m0$B) Incorrectly predicting positive when the actual class is negative$m0$, $m0$C) Incorrectly predicting negative when the actual class is positive$m0$, $m0$D) Correctly predicting the positive class$m0$), 2, $m0$A False Negative occurs when the model predicts negative, but the true label was actually positive — a "missed" positive case.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$What does the F1-Score represent?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The sum of Precision and Recall$m0$, $m0$B) The harmonic mean of Precision and Recall$m0$, $m0$C) The total number of correct predictions$m0$, $m0$D) The difference between Precision and Recall$m0$), 1, $m0$F1-Score combines Precision and Recall using their harmonic mean, providing a single balanced measure of both.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$In medical disease diagnosis, why might Recall often be prioritized over Precision?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) False positives are always more costly than false negatives$m0$, $m0$B) Missing an actual disease case (false negative) can be far more costly than a false alarm$m0$, $m0$C) Recall is always easier to calculate$m0$, $m0$D) Precision cannot be calculated for medical data$m0$), 1, $m0$In many medical contexts, failing to detect a true disease case can have severe consequences, making high Recall (catching as many true cases as possible) especially important.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function calculates Precision?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `accuracy_score()`$m0$, $m0$B) `precision_score()`$m0$, $m0$C) `recall_score()`$m0$, $m0$D) `f1_score()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`precision_score()` is the specific Scikit-learn function for calculating Precision from actual and predicted labels.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$If TP=30, FP=10, what is the Precision?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 0.25$m0$, $m0$B) 0.75$m0$, $m0$C) 0.30$m0$, $m0$D) 1.0$m0$), 1, $m0$Precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 30/(30+10) = 30/40 = 0.75.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$If TP=30, FN=20, what is the Recall?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 0.60$m0$, $m0$B) 0.40$m0$, $m0$C) 0.75$m0$, $m0$D) 1.50$m0$), 0, $m0$Recall = TP/(TP+FN) = 30/(30+20) = 30/50 = 0.60.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-accuracy-precision-recall-f1-score$m0$, $m0$Which scenario would likely justify prioritizing Precision over Recall?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Detecting a rare, highly contagious disease where missing a case is dangerous$m0$, $m0$B) A spam filter, where mistakenly blocking an important legitimate email is costly$m0$, $m0$C) It never makes sense to prioritize one over the other$m0$, $m0$D) Precision should always be ignored in favor of Recall$m0$), 1, $m0$In spam filtering, a false positive (blocking a legitimate email) can be quite costly to the user, making high Precision (avoiding false alarms) particularly valuable.$m0$, 1, 9)
