insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$What is the main purpose of Cross-Validation?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To train a model faster than a single train-test split$m0$, $m0$B) To provide a more reliable, robust estimate of model performance$m0$, $m0$C) To eliminate the need for any test data$m0$, $m0$D) To automatically select the best algorithm$m0$), 1, $m0$Cross-Validation evaluates a model across multiple different train/test combinations, reducing the risk of an unreliable estimate from just one lucky (or unlucky) split.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$In 5-Fold Cross-Validation, how many times is the model trained and tested?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Once$m0$, $m0$B) 5 times$m0$, $m0$C) 10 times$m0$, $m0$D) 25 times$m0$), 1, $m0$With `k=5`, the model is trained and evaluated 5 separate times, each using a different fold as the test set.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$What does Stratified K-Fold specifically ensure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) That each fold has exactly the same number of data points$m0$, $m0$B) That each fold maintains roughly the same class proportions as the full dataset$m0$, $m0$C) That the model trains faster$m0$, $m0$D) That only numeric features are used$m0$), 1, $m0$Stratified K-Fold preserves the original class distribution within each fold, which is especially important for imbalanced classification problems.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function performs Cross-Validation and returns scores for each fold?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `train_test_split()`$m0$, $m0$B) `cross_val_score()`$m0$, $m0$C) `GridSearchCV()`$m0$, $m0$D) `confusion_matrix()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`cross_val_score()` automates the entire Cross-Validation process, returning an array of scores, one per fold.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$Why is it useful to look at the standard deviation of Cross-Validation scores, not just the mean?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Standard deviation is not actually useful in this context$m0$, $m0$B) It reveals how consistent the model's performance is across different folds$m0$, $m0$C) It replaces the need for calculating the mean entirely$m0$, $m0$D) It only applies to regression problems$m0$), 1, $m0$A high standard deviation suggests the model's performance varies significantly depending on which specific data it's trained/tested on, which is valuable additional information beyond the average score alone.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$What is Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation (LOOCV)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A method that uses no test data whatsoever$m0$, $m0$B) A special case of K-Fold where k equals the total number of data points$m0$, $m0$C) A method that only works with exactly 2 folds$m0$, $m0$D) A technique unrelated to Cross-Validation$m0$), 1, $m0$In LOOCV, each "fold" consists of leaving out just a single data point for testing, meaning k equals the size of the dataset.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$Why might Cross-Validation be inappropriate for time-series data without modification?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Cross-Validation always works perfectly for time-series data$m0$, $m0$B) A random fold split can mix future and past data inappropriately, similar to the issue with random train-test splits$m0$, $m0$C) Time-series data cannot be evaluated at all$m0$, $m0$D) Cross-Validation requires categorical data only$m0$), 1, $m0$Just as with a single random train-test split, randomly assigning time-series data to folds can leak future information into training, requiring specialized time-based splitting instead.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$What is a key computational tradeoff of Cross-Validation compared to a single train-test split?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Cross-Validation is always faster$m0$, $m0$B) Cross-Validation requires training the model multiple times, increasing computational cost$m0$, $m0$C) There is no computational difference at all$m0$, $m0$D) Cross-Validation never requires any training$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the model must be trained and evaluated once per fold, Cross-Validation is more computationally expensive than a single train-test split.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class explicitly implements Stratified K-Fold splitting?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `KFold`$m0$, $m0$B) `StratifiedKFold`$m0$, $m0$C) `train_test_split`$m0$, $m0$D) `GridSearchCV`$m0$), 1, $m0$`StratifiedKFold` is the specific Scikit-learn class designed to preserve class proportions across folds.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-cross-validation-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-cross-validation$m0$, $m0$When is Cross-Validation particularly valuable, beyond general model evaluation?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) During hyperparameter tuning, to avoid overfitting to a single validation split$m0$, $m0$B) It has no additional value beyond basic evaluation$m0$, $m0$C) Only when working with unlabeled data$m0$, $m0$D) Only for regression problems, never classification$m0$), 0, $m0$Cross-Validation is especially important during hyperparameter tuning (Module 7), since relying on just one validation split could lead to choosing hyperparameters that only work well for that specific split.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

