values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$What does MAE measure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The average squared difference between actual and predicted values$m0$, $m0$B) The average absolute difference between actual and predicted values$m0$, $m0$C) The proportion of variance explained by the model$m0$, $m0$D) The number of correct classifications$m0$), 1, $m0$MAE calculates the average of the absolute (non-negative) differences between predicted and actual values.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$Why does MSE penalize large errors more heavily than MAE?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) MSE ignores large errors completely$m0$, $m0$B) MSE squares each error before averaging, amplifying the effect of larger deviations$m0$, $m0$C) MSE only considers the smallest errors$m0$, $m0$D) There is no real difference between MAE and MSE$m0$), 1, $m0$Since errors are squared in MSE, larger errors contribute disproportionately more to the final average compared to smaller ones.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$What is RMSE?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The average absolute error$m0$, $m0$B) The square root of MSE$m0$, $m0$C) The proportion of variance explained$m0$, $m0$D) The sum of all errors$m0$), 1, $m0$RMSE is calculated by taking the square root of MSE, bringing the error metric back into the original units of the target variable.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$What does an R² score of 1.0 indicate?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The model performs no better than random guessing$m0$, $m0$B) The model perfectly explains all variance in the target variable$m0$, $m0$C) The model made zero predictions$m0$, $m0$D) The model has 100% classification accuracy$m0$), 1, $m0$An R² of 1.0 means the model's predictions perfectly account for all the variability observed in the actual target values.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$Which metric is generally LESS sensitive to outliers?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) MSE$m0$, $m0$B) RMSE$m0$, $m0$C) MAE$m0$, $m0$D) All three are equally sensitive to outliers$m0$), 2, $m0$Since MAE uses absolute differences rather than squared differences, it doesn't amplify the impact of unusually large errors the way MSE and RMSE do.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn function calculates MAE?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `mean_squared_error()`$m0$, $m0$B) `mean_absolute_error()`$m0$, $m0$C) `r2_score()`$m0$, $m0$D) `accuracy_score()`$m0$), 1, $m0$`mean_absolute_error()` is the specific Scikit-learn function for calculating MAE.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$What is a key advantage of RMSE over raw MSE?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) RMSE is always smaller than MSE$m0$, $m0$B) RMSE is expressed in the same units as the original target variable, making it easier to interpret$m0$, $m0$C) RMSE ignores all large errors$m0$, $m0$D) RMSE cannot be calculated in Python$m0$), 1, $m0$While MSE is in squared units (which can be hard to interpret intuitively), taking its square root (RMSE) restores it to the original, more meaningful units.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$If a model's predictions exactly match all actual values, what would MAE, MSE, and RMSE all equal?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) 1$m0$, $m0$B) 0$m0$, $m0$C) 100$m0$, $m0$D) Undefined$m0$), 1, $m0$With zero difference between every predicted and actual value, all three error metrics (MAE, MSE, RMSE) would equal exactly 0.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$What does R² = 0 indicate about a model?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The model perfectly predicts every value$m0$, $m0$B) The model explains none of the variance, performing no better than simply predicting the mean$m0$, $m0$C) The model has made no predictions at all$m0$, $m0$D) The model's errors are always negative$m0$), 1, $m0$An R² of 0 suggests the model's predictions provide no improvement over simply always predicting the average of the actual values.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r$m0$, $m0$Which regression metric would be most appropriate for a dataset known to contain significant outliers, if you want to avoid letting them dominate the error calculation?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) MSE$m0$, $m0$B) RMSE$m0$, $m0$C) MAE$m0$, $m0$D) All three handle outliers identically$m0$), 2, $m0$Since MAE doesn't square the errors, it's less influenced by a few unusually large deviations compared to MSE or RMSE.$m0$, 1, 9)
