insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What does "bias" refer to in the Bias-Variance Tradeoff?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Error from a model being overly sensitive to training data$m0$, $m0$B) Error from a model making overly simplistic assumptions about the data$m0$, $m0$C) A type of classification metric$m0$, $m0$D) The total number of features used$m0$), 1, $m0$Bias represents systematic error caused by a model that is too simple to capture the true underlying pattern in the data.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What does "variance" refer to in this context?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Error from overly simplistic model assumptions$m0$, $m0$B) Error from a model being overly sensitive to the specific training data used$m0$, $m0$C) The average of all predictions$m0$, $m0$D) A metric used only for classification problems$m0$), 1, $m0$Variance captures how much a model's predictions would change if trained on a different sample of data — high variance means the model is overly tuned to its specific training set.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What typically happens to bias and variance as model complexity increases?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Both bias and variance always increase together$m0$, $m0$B) Bias typically decreases while variance typically increases$m0$, $m0$C) Bias typically increases while variance typically decreases$m0$, $m0$D) Neither bias nor variance is affected by model complexity$m0$), 1, $m0$More complex models can capture more intricate patterns (reducing bias) but become more sensitive to training data specifics/noise (increasing variance).$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What are the symptoms of high bias (underfitting)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Excellent training performance, poor test performance$m0$, $m0$B) Poor performance on both training and test data$m0$, $m0$C) Perfect performance on both training and test data$m0$, $m0$D) No symptoms are observable$m0$), 1, $m0$A high-bias model is too simple to capture genuine patterns, resulting in poor performance across both the training and test sets.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What are the symptoms of high variance (overfitting)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Poor performance on both training and test data$m0$, $m0$B) Excellent training performance but noticeably poorer test performance$m0$, $m0$C) Identical, moderate performance on both sets$m0$, $m0$D) The model refuses to make any predictions$m0$), 1, $m0$A high-variance model fits the training data very closely (including its noise), leading to a significant performance gap when evaluated on new, unseen test data.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$What does the "irreducible error" component represent?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Error that can always be eliminated with a more complex model$m0$, $m0$B) Inherent noise in the data that no model can eliminate$m0$, $m0$C) Error caused specifically by choosing the wrong algorithm$m0$, $m0$D) A type of classification metric$m0$), 1, $m0$Irreducible error reflects fundamental randomness or noise in the data itself, which persists regardless of how good the model is.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a reasonable fix for a model suffering from high bias?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Simplify the model further$m0$, $m0$B) Increase model complexity or add more relevant features$m0$, $m0$C) Remove all training data$m0$, $m0$D) Reduce the amount of test data used$m0$), 1, $m0$Since high bias stems from a model being too simple, increasing its complexity or providing more informative features can help it capture genuine patterns it was previously missing.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a reasonable fix for a model suffering from high variance?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Increase model complexity further$m0$, $m0$B) Simplify the model, gather more training data, or apply regularization$m0$, $m0$C) Remove all test data$m0$, $m0$D) There is no possible fix for high variance$m0$), 1, $m0$Since high variance stems from a model being overly sensitive to specific training data, simplifying it, providing more data, or applying regularization techniques (Module 7) can help reduce this sensitivity.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$How does comparing training and test performance help diagnose bias vs variance issues?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It provides no useful diagnostic information$m0$, $m0$B) A large gap (great training, poor test) suggests high variance; poor performance on both suggests high bias$m0$, $m0$C) Training and test performance are always identical$m0$, $m0$D) This comparison only applies to classification, never regression$m0$), 1, $m0$This train-vs-test comparison is the primary practical signal used to determine whether a model's issue is more likely rooted in bias or variance.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-bias-variance-tradeoff$m0$, $m0$Why is finding the right balance between bias and variance considered a "tradeoff"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Because reducing one generally tends to increase the other, as model complexity changes$m0$, $m0$B) Because bias and variance always move in the exact same direction together$m0$, $m0$C) Because only one of the two ever actually matters$m0$, $m0$D) Because this balance has no relationship to model complexity$m0$), 0, $m0$As model complexity changes, bias and variance typically move in opposite directions, meaning improving one often comes at some cost to the other — hence the need to find a good balance rather than simply minimizing one alone.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

