insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$What is the main purpose of regularization?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To increase model complexity$m0$, $m0$B) To reduce overfitting by penalizing large coefficient values$m0$, $m0$C) To remove all missing values from a dataset$m0$, $m0$D) To speed up data loading$m0$), 1, $m0$Regularization adds a penalty for large coefficients, discouraging overly complex models and helping combat overfitting.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$What is the key difference between L1 and L2 regularization?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are exactly identical in every way$m0$, $m0$B) L1 can shrink coefficients to exactly zero; L2 generally only shrinks them toward zero$m0$, $m0$C) L2 can shrink coefficients to exactly zero; L1 cannot$m0$, $m0$D) Neither method ever changes model coefficients$m0$), 1, $m0$L1's use of absolute values in its penalty allows it to eliminate coefficients entirely, while L2's squared penalty typically only reduces their magnitude without eliminating them.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements L2 (Ridge) regularization for regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `Lasso`$m0$, $m0$B) `Ridge`$m0$, $m0$C) `LogisticRegression`$m0$, $m0$D) `DecisionTreeRegressor`$m0$), 1, $m0$`Ridge` is the Scikit-learn class specifically implementing L2 regularization for regression problems.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$What does the `alpha` hyperparameter control in Ridge/Lasso?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of features in the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) The strength of the regularization penalty$m0$, $m0$C) The number of training examples$m0$, $m0$D) The learning rate of Decision Trees$m0$), 1, $m0$`alpha` determines how strongly the regularization penalty influences the model — higher values mean stronger regularization.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$Why is L1 regularization sometimes described as performing "automatic feature selection"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It always keeps every feature in the model$m0$, $m0$B) It can shrink some coefficients to exactly zero, effectively removing those features$m0$, $m0$C) It requires manual feature removal beforehand$m0$, $m0$D) It has no relationship to feature selection at all$m0$), 1, $m0$By zeroing out certain coefficients entirely, L1 regularization effectively excludes those features from contributing to the model's predictions.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$Why is feature scaling important before applying regularization?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no effect on regularization results$m0$, $m0$B) Regularization penalizes coefficient size, and unscaled features could be unfairly penalized based on their natural scale rather than true importance$m0$, $m0$C) Scaling automatically determines the best alpha value$m0$, $m0$D) Regularization cannot be applied to numeric data without scaling$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the regularization penalty depends directly on coefficient magnitude, differences in feature scale (not true importance) could distort which features get penalized more heavily.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$What happens when `alpha=0` in Ridge or Lasso regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The model becomes equivalent to standard, unregularized Linear Regression$m0$, $m0$B) The model completely ignores the training data$m0$, $m0$C) All coefficients become exactly zero$m0$, $m0$D) The model cannot be trained at all$m0$), 0, $m0$With no regularization penalty applied (alpha=0), Ridge/Lasso behave identically to standard Linear Regression.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$Which regularization method would be more appropriate if you suspect many features in your dataset are irrelevant?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) L2 (Ridge)$m0$, $m0$B) L1 (Lasso)$m0$, $m0$C) Neither method can help with this$m0$, $m0$D) Regularization is irrelevant to feature relevance$m0$), 1, $m0$L1 (Lasso)'s ability to shrink coefficients to exactly zero makes it well-suited for automatically identifying and removing irrelevant features.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$What is a risk of setting `alpha` too high?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The model will always overfit severely$m0$, $m0$B) The model may become too simple, leading to underfitting$m0$, $m0$C) Alpha has no meaningful upper limit or risk$m0$, $m0$D) The model will ignore the regularization penalty entirely$m0$), 1, $m0$Excessively strong regularization can shrink coefficients too aggressively, causing the model to become overly simple and underfit the data.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-regularization-l1-l2-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-regularization-l1-l2$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements L1 (Lasso) regularization for regression?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `Ridge`$m0$, $m0$B) `Lasso`$m0$, $m0$C) `KMeans`$m0$, $m0$D) `DecisionTreeRegressor`$m0$), 1, $m0$`Lasso` is the Scikit-learn class specifically implementing L1 regularization for regression problems.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

