insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$What is the main goal of Hyperparameter Tuning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To automatically clean missing data$m0$, $m0$B) To systematically find the best-performing combination of hyperparameter values$m0$, $m0$C) To remove irrelevant features$m0$, $m0$D) To convert categorical data into numbers$m0$), 1, $m0$Hyperparameter Tuning searches through candidate hyperparameter values to find the combination that yields the best model performance.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$How does Grid Search explore the hyperparameter search space?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) By randomly sampling a subset of combinations$m0$, $m0$B) By exhaustively trying every possible combination$m0$, $m0$C) By only trying the default values$m0$, $m0$D) By ignoring the search space entirely$m0$), 1, $m0$Grid Search systematically tests every single combination defined within the specified search space.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$How does Random Search differ from Grid Search?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It also tries every possible combination$m0$, $m0$B) It samples a random subset of combinations rather than trying all of them$m0$, $m0$C) It doesn't use Cross-Validation at all$m0$, $m0$D) It cannot be used with Scikit-learn$m0$), 1, $m0$Random Search selects a random subset of hyperparameter combinations to evaluate, rather than exhaustively testing every one.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$Why is Cross-Validation typically used alongside Hyperparameter Tuning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real benefit in this context$m0$, $m0$B) It provides a more robust evaluation of each candidate combination, rather than relying on a single validation split$m0$, $m0$C) Cross-Validation replaces the need for hyperparameters entirely$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with Grid Search, never Random Search$m0$), 1, $m0$Combining tuning with Cross-Validation ensures the chosen hyperparameters generalize well, rather than just fitting one particular validation split by chance.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$Which Scikit-learn class implements Grid Search combined with Cross-Validation?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `RandomizedSearchCV`$m0$, $m0$B) `GridSearchCV`$m0$, $m0$C) `cross_val_score`$m0$, $m0$D) `train_test_split`$m0$), 1, $m0$`GridSearchCV` performs an exhaustive search over the specified parameter grid, using Cross-Validation to evaluate each combination.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$What does the `n_iter` parameter control in `RandomizedSearchCV`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The number of features in the dataset$m0$, $m0$B) The number of random hyperparameter combinations to try$m0$, $m0$C) The number of cross-validation folds$m0$, $m0$D) The maximum depth of a Decision Tree$m0$), 1, $m0$`n_iter` specifies how many randomly sampled hyperparameter combinations `RandomizedSearchCV` should evaluate.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$What is a key computational advantage of Random Search over Grid Search?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Random Search always finds a strictly better result$m0$, $m0$B) Random Search can be much faster, especially with large search spaces, since it doesn't test every combination$m0$, $m0$C) Random Search requires no computation at all$m0$, $m0$D) Grid Search is always faster in every scenario$m0$), 1, $m0$By sampling only a subset of combinations, Random Search can significantly reduce computational cost compared to exhaustively testing every combination in a large search space.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$What does `grid_search.best_params_` return after fitting?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The worst-performing hyperparameter combination$m0$, $m0$B) The hyperparameter combination that achieved the best Cross-Validation score$m0$, $m0$C) The raw training data$m0$, $m0$D) The total number of combinations tested$m0$), 1, $m0$`best_params_` reveals which specific combination of hyperparameter values produced the best average performance during the search.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$Why should final model evaluation happen on a separate test set, never used during hyperparameter tuning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It doesn't matter which data is used for final evaluation$m0$, $m0$B) Using the same data for tuning and final evaluation could give an overly optimistic, biased performance estimate$m0$, $m0$C) Test sets are never needed once tuning is complete$m0$, $m0$D) Final evaluation should always use the exact same data as tuning$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the tuning process specifically selects hyperparameters that perform well on the validation data used during search, evaluating on that same data again would provide an unfairly optimistic result — a genuinely separate test set is needed for honest final evaluation.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-hyperparameter-tuning$m0$, $m0$Which scenario would most strongly favor using Random Search over Grid Search?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Tuning just one hyperparameter with 2 candidate values$m0$, $m0$B) Tuning five different hyperparameters, each with many candidate values, creating an enormous search space$m0$, $m0$C) A situation with no computational constraints whatsoever$m0$, $m0$D) A situation where Cross-Validation is not being used$m0$), 1, $m0$With a very large search space (many hyperparameters and many candidate values each), Grid Search's exhaustive approach becomes computationally impractical, making Random Search's sampling approach much more attractive.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

