values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `np.random.default_rng(42)` provide?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A random result that changes every time the code runs$m0$, $m0$B) A reproducible random number generator seeded with 42$m0$, $m0$C) A way to remove missing values$m0$, $m0$D) A method for scaling features$m0$), 1, $m0$Seeding the generator with a fixed value (42) ensures the exact same sequence of "random" values is produced every time the code runs.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `scores[scores >= 50]` demonstrate?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Fancy indexing$m0$, $m0$B) Boolean masking$m0$, $m0$C) Matrix inversion$m0$, $m0$D) Reproducible randomness$m0$), 1, $m0$This filters the array based on a condition (`scores >= 50`), which is the defining characteristic of boolean masking.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `scores[[2, 4, 1]]` demonstrate?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Boolean masking$m0$, $m0$B) Fancy indexing$m0$, $m0$C) A random number generation$m0$, $m0$D) A linear algebra operation$m0$), 1, $m0$This selects elements at specific index positions (2, 4, and 1, in that order), which is fancy indexing.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Which NumPy submodule provides matrix inverse and eigenvalue calculations?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `np.random`$m0$, $m0$B) `np.linalg`$m0$, $m0$C) `np.stats`$m0$, $m0$D) `np.metrics`$m0$), 1, $m0$`np.linalg` houses NumPy's linear algebra functions, including matrix inversion (`inv`) and eigenvalue decomposition (`eig`).$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Why is reproducible randomness important in ML experiments?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real importance$m0$, $m0$B) It ensures consistent, comparable results when code is re-run or shared with others$m0$, $m0$C) It automatically improves model accuracy$m0$, $m0$D) It removes the need for a test set$m0$), 1, $m0$Fixing the random seed/generator ensures that anyone re-running the same code gets identical results, which is essential for debugging and fair comparison.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What underlies a Pandas DataFrame internally, column by column?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Python lists exclusively$m0$, $m0$B) NumPy arrays$m0$, $m0$C) SQL tables$m0$, $m0$D) JSON objects$m0$), 1, $m0$Each column of a Pandas DataFrame is internally represented and processed using NumPy arrays.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What connects NumPy's `np.linalg.eig()` function to Module 5's content?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no connection to any previous module$m0$, $m0$B) Eigenvalues/eigenvectors are the mathematical basis for PCA (Principal Component Analysis)$m0$, $m0$C) It's used exclusively for clustering$m0$, $m0$D) It's used only for data cleaning$m0$), 1, $m0$PCA (Module 5, Topic 5) relies on eigenvalue/eigenvector decomposition of the data's covariance matrix, directly connecting to this NumPy function.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Why might the modern `np.random.default_rng()` be preferred over the older `np.random.seed()`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `default_rng()` is slower and less reliable$m0$, $m0$B) `default_rng()` provides more explicit, contained reproducibility, avoiding subtle global-state bugs in larger codebases$m0$, $m0$C) `np.random.seed()` cannot be used at all in modern NumPy$m0$, $m0$D) There is no meaningful difference between them$m0$), 1, $m0$`default_rng()` creates an explicit, localized generator object rather than relying on NumPy's older global random state, which can cause unpredictable interactions in complex code.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `.to_numpy()` do when called on a Pandas DataFrame?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It removes all missing values$m0$, $m0$B) It returns the underlying data as a NumPy array$m0$, $m0$C) It converts the DataFrame into a dictionary$m0$, $m0$D) It scales all numeric columns$m0$), 1, $m0$`.to_numpy()` extracts the DataFrame's underlying data directly as a NumPy array, revealing the structure Pandas is built upon.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-numpy-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Which scenario would use boolean masking rather than fancy indexing?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Selecting the 1st, 3rd, and 5th elements specifically$m0$, $m0$B) Selecting all elements greater than a certain threshold value$m0$, $m0$C) Reversing the order of an array$m0$, $m0$D) Calculating a matrix's determinant$m0$), 1, $m0$Selecting elements based on a condition (like "greater than a threshold") is precisely what boolean masking is designed for.$m0$, 1, 9)
