values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What pattern does `groupby()` implement?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Sort-filter-display$m0$, $m0$B) Split-apply-combine$m0$, $m0$C) Load-clean-save$m0$, $m0$D) Encode-scale-train$m0$), 1, $m0$`groupby()` splits data into groups, applies an aggregation function to each, then combines the results into a summary.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `pd.merge(df1, df2, on="id", how="inner")` do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Keeps only rows where "id" matches in both DataFrames$m0$, $m0$B) Keeps all rows regardless of whether "id" matches$m0$, $m0$C) Deletes the "id" column from both DataFrames$m0$, $m0$D) Randomly combines rows from both DataFrames$m0$), 0, $m0$An "inner" merge keeps only the rows where the key column ("id") has matching values present in BOTH DataFrames.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `pivot_table()` primarily do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Removes missing values$m0$, $m0$B) Reshapes data into a cross-tabulated summary across two categorical dimensions$m0$, $m0$C) Combines two DataFrames based on a key$m0$, $m0$D) Scales numeric features$m0$), 1, $m0$`pivot_table()` summarizes a value across combinations of two (or more) categorical variables, presented as a reshaped table.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What does `df["column"].apply(lambda x: ...)` do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Removes the column entirely$m0$, $m0$B) Applies a custom function to every value in that column$m0$, $m0$C) Merges the column with another DataFrame$m0$, $m0$D) Automatically scales the column's values$m0$), 1, $m0$`apply()` runs the given function (often a `lambda`) against every individual value in the column.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Which `merge()` `how` option keeps ALL rows from both DataFrames, filling unmatched values with NaN?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `"inner"`$m0$, $m0$B) `"left"`$m0$, $m0$C) `"outer"`$m0$, $m0$D) `"right"`$m0$), 2, $m0$An "outer" merge preserves every row from both DataFrames, using NaN wherever no match exists on the other side.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Why might `apply()` be slower than a built-in Pandas/NumPy operation for the same task?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `apply()` is always faster than any alternative$m0$, $m0$B) `apply()` runs a Python function individually, without the internal optimizations of vectorized built-in operations$m0$, $m0$C) `apply()` cannot process numeric data$m0$, $m0$D) There is no performance difference at all$m0$), 1, $m0$Built-in vectorized operations are optimized internally (often in C), while `apply()` calls a Python function repeatedly, which is generally slower for large datasets.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What must follow a `groupby()` call to actually produce a usable result?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Nothing — `groupby()` alone produces the final result$m0$, $m0$B) An aggregation function, such as `.mean()`, `.sum()`, or `.count()`$m0$, $m0$C) A `merge()` call$m0$, $m0$D) A `pivot_table()` call$m0$), 1, $m0$`groupby()` alone returns a special grouped object; an aggregation function is needed to actually compute and combine the summarized results.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$Which `merge()` `how` option keeps all rows from the LEFT DataFrame, matching where possible?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `"inner"`$m0$, $m0$B) `"left"`$m0$, $m0$C) `"outer"`$m0$, $m0$D) `"right"`$m0$), 1, $m0$A "left" merge preserves every row from the left (first) DataFrame, filling in matches from the right DataFrame where available.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$What is a common real-world reason to use `merge()`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To remove all missing values from a single DataFrame$m0$, $m0$B) To combine data from multiple related sources/tables sharing a common key$m0$, $m0$C) To calculate summary statistics for a single column$m0$, $m0$D) To visualize data as a chart$m0$), 1, $m0$Real-world data is often split across multiple tables (e.g., orders and customers), and `merge()` combines them based on a shared identifier.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pandas-condensed-reference$m0$, $m0$When is `apply()` most appropriately used?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Whenever any operation is needed, regardless of alternatives$m0$, $m0$B) When a genuinely custom transformation is needed that built-in vectorized functions don't directly support$m0$, $m0$C) Only for removing missing values$m0$, $m0$D) Only for merging DataFrames$m0$), 1, $m0$Since `apply()` carries a performance cost compared to built-in vectorized operations, it's best reserved for cases where no faster built-in alternative achieves the same custom logic.$m0$, 1, 9)
