  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What is a "kernel" in Jupyter Notebook?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A type of chart$m0$, $m0$B) The background process that executes code and maintains variable state$m0$, $m0$C) A Markdown formatting tag$m0$, $m0$D) A file format for saving notebooks$m0$), 1, $m0$The kernel is the running process that executes code cells and keeps variables in memory across the session.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What is the purpose of a Markdown cell?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To execute Python code$m0$, $m0$B) To display formatted text, headings, and documentation alongside code$m0$, $m0$C) To store the kernel's variables$m0$, $m0$D) To automatically generate charts$m0$), 1, $m0$Markdown cells render formatted text (headings, bullet points, etc.), used for documenting and explaining an analysis.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$Why can a later cell use a variable defined in an earlier cell without redefining it?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) This is not actually possible in Jupyter$m0$, $m0$B) The kernel maintains persistent variable state across all cells in the session$m0$, $m0$C) Variables are automatically reset before each cell$m0$, $m0$D) Only Markdown cells can share variables$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the kernel keeps variables in memory for the entire session, any cell can access variables defined in any previously run cell.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What does the `%matplotlib inline` magic command do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It deletes all charts from the notebook$m0$, $m0$B) It ensures Matplotlib charts display directly within the notebook's output$m0$, $m0$C) It installs Matplotlib$m0$, $m0$D) It converts charts into Markdown text$m0$), 1, $m0$This magic command configures Matplotlib to render its output directly inline within the notebook, rather than in a separate window.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What is a potential risk of running Jupyter cells out of their original top-to-bottom order?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) There is no risk whatsoever$m0$, $m0$B) It can lead to confusing bugs and a kernel state that doesn't match what the notebook visually appears to show$m0$, $m0$C) Cells can never be run out of order in Jupyter$m0$, $m0$D) It automatically corrupts the saved file$m0$), 1, $m0$Since the kernel's actual state depends on the ACTUAL order cells were run (not their visual position in the notebook), running them out of sequence can create confusing, hard-to-reproduce situations.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What does "Restart Kernel and Run All" help verify?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) That the notebook's code runs correctly and reproducibly from a clean state, top to bottom$m0$, $m0$B) That all Markdown cells are properly formatted$m0$, $m0$C) That the file has been saved$m0$, $m0$D) That the correct version of Python is installed$m0$), 0, $m0$This action clears all existing variable state and re-runs every cell from the top, confirming the notebook works reproducibly as a whole, not just in whatever ad-hoc order it happened to be run before.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$What file extension do Jupyter Notebooks use?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) `.py`$m0$, $m0$B) `.ipynb`$m0$, $m0$C) `.md`$m0$, $m0$D) `.json`$m0$), 1, $m0$Jupyter Notebooks are saved with the `.ipynb` extension, a JSON-based format storing both code and output.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a common best practice for working in Jupyter Notebook?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Writing one giant cell containing the entire analysis$m0$, $m0$B) Keeping cells small and focused, with Markdown cells documenting reasoning$m0$, $m0$C) Never using Markdown cells$m0$, $m0$D) Avoiding restarting the kernel under any circumstances$m0$), 1, $m0$Breaking work into small, focused cells (with clear documentation) makes notebooks easier to understand, debug, and maintain.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-jupyter-notebook-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-jupyter-notebook$m0$, $m0$Why might Jupyter Notebook be less suited than a `.py` script for production, automated ML pipelines?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Notebooks cannot contain any Python code$m0$, $m0$B) Their interactive, cell-by-cell execution model is less predictable and harder to integrate into automated, always-top-to-bottom workflows$m0$, $m0$C) `.py` scripts cannot use Pandas or Scikit-learn$m0$, $m0$D) There is no meaningful difference between the two for production use$m0$), 1, $m0$Production pipelines typically need consistent, fully automated, top-to-bottom execution — a script's straightforward execution model fits this better than a notebook's flexible, potentially out-of-order cell execution.$m0$, 1, 8),
