  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$What are the two key factors that made Deep Learning practically feasible in recent years?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Cheaper keyboards and mice$m0$, $m0$B) Large datasets and powerful (GPU) computing$m0$, $m0$C) Faster internet browsers only$m0$, $m0$D) Smaller, simpler datasets$m0$), 1, $m0$Deep Learning's core ideas are decades old, but only became practically powerful once massive datasets and affordable GPU computing power became widely available.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$What does "hierarchical feature learning" mean?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Features are chosen randomly with no structure$m0$, $m0$B) Early layers learn simple patterns, which later layers combine into increasingly complex, meaningful patterns$m0$, $m0$C) All layers learn the exact same features$m0$, $m0$D) Only the final layer learns anything useful$m0$), 1, $m0$Deep networks build understanding progressively, layer by layer, from simple low-level patterns to complex, abstract ones.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$Which type of data does Deep Learning typically excel at, compared to traditional ML?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Small, simple spreadsheets only$m0$, $m0$B) Unstructured data like images, audio, and text$m0$, $m0$C) Data with fewer than 10 rows$m0$, $m0$D) Only categorical data$m0$), 1, $m0$Deep Learning's ability to automatically learn features from raw data makes it especially powerful for complex, unstructured data types.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$Why might traditional ML still be preferred for structured, tabular data?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Traditional ML never works on tabular data$m0$, $m0$B) It's often faster to train, more interpretable, and performs comparably well on structured data$m0$, $m0$C) Deep Learning cannot process numbers$m0$, $m0$D) Traditional ML always requires more data than Deep Learning$m0$), 1, $m0$For smaller, structured datasets, traditional ML models often match Deep Learning's performance while being faster and easier to interpret.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$What is a common limitation of Deep Learning models?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are always easy to interpret$m0$, $m0$B) They can be considered "black box" models, harder to interpret than simpler traditional ML models$m0$, $m0$C) They never require any data$m0$, $m0$D) They cannot be used for image recognition$m0$), 1, $m0$Due to their many layers and complex internal representations, Deep Learning models are generally much harder to interpret than models like Decision Trees or Linear Regression.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a real-world Deep Learning application?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A simple spreadsheet formula$m0$, $m0$B) A voice assistant recognizing spoken commands$m0$, $m0$C) Manually sorting a list of numbers$m0$, $m0$D) A basic if-else rule-based program$m0$), 1, $m0$Voice assistants rely on Deep Learning models to process raw audio and recognize speech patterns.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$What is the relationship between Deep Learning and Machine Learning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are completely unrelated fields$m0$, $m0$B) Deep Learning is a subfield of Machine Learning$m0$, $m0$C) Machine Learning is a subfield of Deep Learning$m0$, $m0$D) They are exactly the same thing with different names$m0$), 1, $m0$As established in Module 2, Deep Learning is a specific approach within the broader field of Machine Learning, using layered neural networks.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$Why does Deep Learning typically require large amounts of data?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It doesn't actually require much data at all$m0$, $m0$B) Learning complex, hierarchical patterns automatically (without manual feature engineering) generally requires many examples$m0$, $m0$C) Small datasets always work better with Deep Learning$m0$, $m0$D) Data requirements have no relationship to Deep Learning's approach$m0$), 1, $m0$Since Deep Learning must discover useful features on its own rather than relying on human-engineered ones, it typically needs substantially more examples to learn reliably.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$What role do GPUs typically play in Deep Learning?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They have no relevance to Deep Learning$m0$, $m0$B) They significantly accelerate the heavy computations required to train Deep Learning models$m0$, $m0$C) They are only used for playing video games$m0$, $m0$D) They replace the need for any training data$m0$), 1, $m0$GPUs are optimized for the kind of parallel numerical computation Deep Learning models require, dramatically speeding up training compared to standard CPUs.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-deep-learning-basics-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-deep-learning-basics$m0$, $m0$Which of these topics, covered later in this module, directly builds on Deep Learning fundamentals?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Basic Statistics$m0$, $m0$B) Computer Vision and NLP$m0$, $m0$C) Data Cleaning$m0$, $m0$D) Simple Linear Regression$m0$), 1, $m0$Computer Vision (Topic 5) and NLP (Topic 6), along with Generative AI and LLMs, are all specialized applications built on Deep Learning foundations.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

