insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What does a single neuron compute?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Only a raw sum of its inputs, with no further processing$m0$, $m0$B) A weighted sum of inputs plus a bias, passed through an activation function$m0$, $m0$C) The average of all training data$m0$, $m0$D) A random number unrelated to its inputs$m0$), 1, $m0$A neuron multiplies each input by its corresponding weight, sums these, adds a bias, and applies a non-linear activation function to produce its output.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What is the purpose of an activation function?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To slow down training unnecessarily$m0$, $m0$B) To introduce non-linearity, enabling the network to learn complex patterns beyond simple straight lines$m0$, $m0$C) To remove all weights from the network$m0$, $m0$D) To convert the network into a Decision Tree$m0$), 1, $m0$Without a non-linear activation function, stacking multiple layers would mathematically collapse into an equivalent single linear model, severely limiting what the network could learn.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What is "forward propagation"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Adjusting weights based on calculated error$m0$, $m0$B) Passing input data through the network's layers to produce a final output$m0$, $m0$C) Removing neurons from a network$m0$, $m0$D) A type of activation function$m0$), 1, $m0$Forward propagation is the process of data flowing from the input layer, through hidden layers, to produce the network's final output/prediction.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What does "backpropagation" do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It passes data forward through the network only$m0$, $m0$B) It calculates the error and adjusts weights backward through the network to reduce that error$m0$, $m0$C) It removes all hidden layers$m0$, $m0$D) It has no relationship to training a neural network$m0$), 1, $m0$Backpropagation works backward from the calculated loss (error), determining how much each weight contributed to that error, and adjusting weights accordingly.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What does "deep" refer to in "Deep Learning"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A very deep understanding of statistics$m0$, $m0$B) Having many hidden layers in a neural network$m0$, $m0$C) A single-layer network only$m0$, $m0$D) A network with no activation functions$m0$), 1, $m0$"Deep" specifically refers to neural networks with multiple (often many) hidden layers, as opposed to a simple, shallow network.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$Which activation function is commonly used in hidden layers and outputs 0 for negative inputs?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Sigmoid$m0$, $m0$B) Softmax$m0$, $m0$C) ReLU$m0$, $m0$D) Linear$m0$), 2, $m0$ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) outputs the input directly if it's positive, and 0 otherwise, making it a very common choice for hidden layers.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$Which activation function is commonly used in the output layer for binary classification?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) ReLU$m0$, $m0$B) Sigmoid$m0$, $m0$C) A linear function with no bounds$m0$, $m0$D) None — output layers never use activation functions$m0$), 1, $m0$Sigmoid squashes output into a range between 0 and 1, representing a probability — the same function used in Logistic Regression (Module 4, Topic 3).$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What role does the "loss function" play in training a neural network?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no role in training$m0$, $m0$B) It measures how far the network's prediction is from the actual correct answer, guiding the backpropagation process$m0$, $m0$C) It replaces the need for an activation function$m0$, $m0$D) It only applies to unsupervised learning$m0$), 1, $m0$The loss function quantifies prediction error, and this error signal is exactly what backpropagation uses to determine how to adjust the network's weights.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$What would happen to a multi-layer network WITHOUT any activation functions?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It would become even more powerful than with activation functions$m0$, $m0$B) It would mathematically behave equivalent to a single linear model, regardless of how many layers it has$m0$, $m0$C) It would automatically prevent overfitting$m0$, $m0$D) It would function identically to a Decision Tree$m0$), 1, $m0$Without non-linear activation functions, stacking layers together mathematically simplifies down to a single linear transformation, losing the network's ability to model complex, non-linear relationships.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-neural-networks-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-neural-networks$m0$, $m0$Which of these best describes the input layer of a neural network?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It applies backpropagation$m0$, $m0$B) It receives the raw input data before any hidden layer processing occurs$m0$, $m0$C) It always produces the network's final prediction$m0$, $m0$D) It contains no neurons at all$m0$), 1, $m0$The input layer is simply where raw data (e.g., pixel values or numeric features) enters the network, before being processed by any hidden layers.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

