insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What is the relationship between TensorFlow and Keras?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are completely unrelated libraries$m0$, $m0$B) Keras is a high-level, simplified interface built on top of TensorFlow$m0$, $m0$C) TensorFlow is built on top of Keras$m0$, $m0$D) They are exactly the same, just with different names$m0$), 1, $m0$Keras provides a simpler, more readable API for building and training neural networks, while TensorFlow provides the underlying computational engine.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What is a "tensor"?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A type of Decision Tree$m0$, $m0$B) A multi-dimensional array of numbers, similar to a NumPy array but capable of running on GPUs$m0$, $m0$C) A single Python variable holding text$m0$, $m0$D) A type of activation function$m0$), 1, $m0$Tensors are the fundamental data structure in TensorFlow, conceptually similar to NumPy arrays but optimized for GPU computation.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What does a `Dense` layer mean in Keras?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A layer with no neurons at all$m0$, $m0$B) A fully connected layer, where every neuron connects to every neuron in the previous layer$m0$, $m0$C) A layer used only for image data$m0$, $m0$D) A layer that removes missing values$m0$), 1, $m0$"Dense" specifically refers to a fully connected layer, the standard layer type where every input connects to every neuron.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What does `model.compile()` configure?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The raw training data$m0$, $m0$B) The loss function, optimizer, and metrics to use during training$m0$, $m0$C) The number of epochs only$m0$, $m0$D) The final predictions$m0$), 1, $m0$`.compile()` sets up how the model will learn — specifying the loss function (error measurement), the optimizer (learning algorithm), and any metrics to track.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$Which activation function would be appropriate for the output layer of a binary classification network?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) ReLU$m0$, $m0$B) Sigmoid$m0$, $m0$C) None — output layers never need activation functions$m0$, $m0$D) A random function$m0$), 1, $m0$Sigmoid squashes output into a 0-1 probability range, appropriate for binary classification, mirroring Logistic Regression's approach.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What does an "epoch" refer to in the context of `.fit()`?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A single training example$m0$, $m0$B) One complete pass through the entire training dataset$m0$, $m0$C) A type of activation function$m0$, $m0$D) The final test accuracy$m0$), 1, $m0$An epoch represents one full cycle of forward propagation, loss calculation, and backpropagation across the entire training set.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What does `model.evaluate(X_test, y_test)` do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Trains the model further on test data$m0$, $m0$B) Assesses the trained model's performance on the test set, reporting loss and specified metrics$m0$, $m0$C) Deletes the trained model$m0$, $m0$D) Changes the model's architecture$m0$), 1, $m0$`.evaluate()` measures how well the already-trained model performs on the given (typically held-out) test data.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$Why is "adam" a commonly used choice for the optimizer parameter?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It is the only optimizer that exists$m0$, $m0$B) It's a well-established, generally effective default choice for many problems$m0$, $m0$C) It removes the need for a loss function$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with image data$m0$), 1, $m0$Adam is a widely used, generally reliable optimization algorithm that tends to work well as a solid default across many different types of neural network problems.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$What is a common mistake when building a Keras model for multi-class classification (e.g., 10 classes)?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Using ReLU in hidden layers$m0$, $m0$B) Using a single output neuron with Sigmoid, instead of multiple neurons with Softmax$m0$, $m0$C) Compiling the model before fitting it$m0$, $m0$D) Using the `Sequential` API$m0$), 1, $m0$Multi-class classification requires an output layer with one neuron per class, using Softmax activation — using a single Sigmoid neuron (appropriate only for binary classification) would be a mismatch.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-tensorflow-keras-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-tensorflow-keras$m0$, $m0$Which of these best summarizes the typical Keras workflow?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Predict → Train → Compile$m0$, $m0$B) Build architecture → Compile → Fit → Predict/Evaluate$m0$, $m0$C) Evaluate → Build → Compile$m0$, $m0$D) There is no consistent workflow in Keras$m0$), 1, $m0$This reflects the standard sequence: first define the network's structure, then configure how it will learn, then train it, then use it to predict or assess its performance.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

