select $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$PyTorch — Quick Test$m0$, 'aptitude', 10, 10,
  $m0$A 10-question multiple-choice check on PyTorch.$m0$,
  10, 4, true, 'Easy',
  g.id, tp.id, 'published', false,
  0, $m0$Answer all 10 questions. Each carries 1 mark. No negative marking.$m0$, true, true
from g, tp
on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What is PyTorch primarily known for?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Being exclusively for traditional ML, not Deep Learning$m0$, $m0$B) Its flexible, explicit, Python-native style for building and training neural networks$m0$, $m0$C) Having no relationship to neural networks whatsoever$m0$, $m0$D) Requiring no code at all to build a model$m0$), 1, $m0$PyTorch is well known for its explicit, code-first, "Python-like" approach to defining and training Deep Learning models.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What does PyTorch's `autograd` system do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It automatically writes the entire training loop for you$m0$, $m0$B) It automatically tracks operations on tensors and computes the gradients needed for backpropagation$m0$, $m0$C) It removes the need for any loss function$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with image data$m0$), 1, $m0$`autograd` handles the complex calculus behind backpropagation automatically, tracking every operation so gradients can be computed without manual derivation.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$How are neural networks typically defined in PyTorch?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) As a simple list of layers, with no further code required$m0$, $m0$B) As a Python class inheriting from `nn.Module`, with `__init__` and `forward` methods$m0$, $m0$C) Networks cannot be explicitly defined in PyTorch$m0$, $m0$D) Only through a graphical drag-and-drop interface$m0$), 1, $m0$PyTorch's standard approach defines a network as a class, using `__init__` to set up layers and `forward` to describe how data flows through them.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What does `optimizer.zero_grad()` do, and why is it important?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It has no real purpose and can be safely skipped$m0$, $m0$B) It resets accumulated gradients before each new backward pass, preventing incorrect gradient accumulation$m0$, $m0$C) It deletes the entire model$m0$, $m0$D) It performs the forward pass$m0$), 1, $m0$Since PyTorch accumulates gradients by default, forgetting to reset them before each new backward pass would cause incorrect, compounding gradient calculations.$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What does `loss.backward()` trigger?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) A forward pass through the network$m0$, $m0$B) The autograd system calculating gradients for backpropagation$m0$, $m0$C) The deletion of the loss function$m0$, $m0$D) Model evaluation on test data$m0$), 1, $m0$`.backward()` triggers PyTorch's autograd system to compute the gradients needed to update each weight during backpropagation.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What is a key difference between PyTorch's and Keras's training approach?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They are identical in every way$m0$, $m0$B) PyTorch typically requires writing an explicit, manual training loop, while Keras's `.fit()` handles this internally$m0$, $m0$C) Keras cannot train any neural network$m0$, $m0$D) PyTorch does not support GPUs$m0$), 1, $m0$This is the key practical distinction discussed in this topic — PyTorch's more explicit style versus Keras's higher-level, automated `.fit()` approach.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$Why is PyTorch particularly popular in AI research?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It cannot be used for any serious research work$m0$, $m0$B) Its flexible, explicit style makes it well-suited for implementing novel, custom architectures$m0$, $m0$C) It has no relationship to Deep Learning$m0$, $m0$D) It only works with pre-built, fixed architectures$m0$), 1, $m0$Researchers often need fine-grained control to implement new, experimental ideas, which PyTorch's explicit style readily supports.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$What does the `forward` method in a PyTorch `nn.Module` class define?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) The layers available to the network$m0$, $m0$B) How input data actually flows through the defined layers to produce an output$m0$, $m0$C) The optimizer to be used$m0$, $m0$D) The loss function$m0$), 1, $m0$While `__init__` sets up which layers exist, `forward` explicitly describes the actual computation — how data moves through those layers.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$Which of these is a common mistake when writing a PyTorch training loop?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Calling `loss.backward()` at all$m0$, $m0$B) Forgetting to call `optimizer.zero_grad()` before each new backward pass$m0$, $m0$C) Defining the network as a class$m0$, $m0$D) Using an optimizer at all$m0$), 1, $m0$Omitting `zero_grad()` causes gradients to accumulate incorrectly across training iterations, a very common beginner mistake in PyTorch.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-pytorch-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-pytorch$m0$, $m0$Which framework might a beginner reasonably start with, given its gentler learning curve?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) PyTorch, due to its more manual training loop$m0$, $m0$B) TensorFlow/Keras, due to its higher-level, simplified `.fit()`-based workflow$m0$, $m0$C) Neither framework is suitable for beginners$m0$, $m0$D) There is no meaningful difference in learning curve between the two$m0$), 1, $m0$Keras's higher-level abstractions (like `.fit()`) generally offer a gentler introduction to Deep Learning before moving to PyTorch's more explicit style.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

