insert into public.test_questions (id, test_id, question_text, options, correct_index, explanation, marks, sort_order)
values
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-1$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$How is a grayscale image typically represented numerically?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) As a single number$m0$, $m0$B) As a 2D grid of pixel brightness values$m0$, $m0$C) As a text string$m0$, $m0$D) As a categorical label only$m0$), 1, $m0$A grayscale image is represented as a 2D grid, where each value indicates the brightness of that specific pixel.$m0$, 1, 0),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-2$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$What does a "convolution" operation do?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It removes all pixels from an image$m0$, $m0$B) It slides a small filter across an image, detecting how well local patches match a specific pattern$m0$, $m0$C) It converts an image into text$m0$, $m0$D) It always deletes the image entirely$m0$), 1, $m0$Convolution systematically applies a learned filter across the image, identifying where specific patterns (like edges) appear.$m0$, 1, 1),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-3$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$What is the purpose of a "pooling" layer?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) To increase the image's resolution$m0$, $m0$B) To shrink the representation while preserving the most important information$m0$, $m0$C) To add more color channels to the image$m0$, $m0$D) To remove all convolutional layers$m0$), 1, $m0$Pooling summarizes small regions (e.g., taking the maximum value), reducing the amount of data while keeping key information intact.$m0$, 1, 2),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-4$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$Which CV task would answer the question "What objects are in this image, and roughly where are they located?"$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Image Classification$m0$, $m0$B) Object Detection$m0$, $m0$C) None of these tasks address this$m0$, $m0$D) Basic pixel counting$m0$), 1, $m0$Object Detection specifically identifies both WHAT objects are present and WHERE they are located (via bounding boxes).$m0$, 1, 3),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-5$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$Which CV task provides the most precise, pixel-by-pixel understanding of object boundaries?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Image Classification$m0$, $m0$B) Object Detection$m0$, $m0$C) Image Segmentation$m0$, $m0$D) None of these tasks provide pixel-level detail$m0$), 2, $m0$Image Segmentation labels each individual pixel according to which object (or background) it belongs to, providing far more precision than a simple bounding box.$m0$, 1, 4),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-6$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$Why is convolution more efficient than a standard fully-connected layer for processing images?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) It requires more weights overall$m0$, $m0$B) The same small filter is reused across the entire image, rather than needing separate weights for every pixel position$m0$, $m0$C) It cannot detect any patterns at all$m0$, $m0$D) It only works on very small images$m0$), 1, $m0$Reusing the same filter across all positions in the image dramatically reduces the number of weights needed, compared to connecting every pixel individually to every neuron.$m0$, 1, 5),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-7$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$What is "transfer learning" in the context of Computer Vision?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Training a completely new model from scratch every single time$m0$, $m0$B) Starting from a large, pretrained model and fine-tuning it on your specific dataset$m0$, $m0$C) A technique that has no relevance to Computer Vision$m0$, $m0$D) Transferring images between different file formats$m0$), 1, $m0$Transfer learning leverages an existing, already-trained model's learned features, adapting them to a new, specific task — often far more practical than training entirely from scratch.$m0$, 1, 6),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-8$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$What do early convolutional layers in a CNN typically learn to detect?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Complete, complex objects immediately$m0$, $m0$B) Simple patterns like edges and basic textures$m0$, $m0$C) Only text characters$m0$, $m0$D) Nothing at all — only later layers do any learning$m0$), 1, $m0$Following hierarchical feature learning (Topic 1), early layers detect simple, low-level patterns, which later layers combine into increasingly complex representations.$m0$, 1, 7),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-9$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$Why do color images typically have 3 channels?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) Because color has no relationship to channels$m0$, $m0$B) They represent Red, Green, and Blue intensity values, which combine to form the full color image$m0$, $m0$C) Because all images must have exactly 3 pixels$m0$, $m0$D) Channels only apply to grayscale images$m0$), 1, $m0$The standard RGB color model represents each color image as three separate grids (channels) — one each for red, green, and blue intensity.$m0$, 1, 8),
  ($m0$tq-aiml-computer-vision-10$m0$, $m0$mt-aiml-computer-vision$m0$, $m0$What is a limitation of CNNs mentioned in this topic?$m0$, jsonb_build_array($m0$A) They can never be fooled or tricked in any way$m0$, $m0$B) They can be vulnerable to adversarial examples — subtly modified images crafted to cause incorrect predictions$m0$, $m0$C) They require no training data whatsoever$m0$, $m0$D) They cannot process color images$m0$), 1, $m0$Adversarial examples are specially crafted, often barely perceptible image modifications designed specifically to trick a CNN into making an incorrect prediction.$m0$, 1, 9)
on conflict (id) do nothing;

